1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,587
Chapter 5: Implantation and Placental Development.

3
00:00:06,587 --> 00:00:25,912
Ovarian–Endometrial Cycle Decidua Implantation And Early Trophoblast Formation Placenta And Chorion Amnion Umbilical Cord Placental Hormones Fetal Adrenal Gland–Placental Interactions References All obstetricians should understand the basic biological steps required for women to achieve pregnancy.

4
00:00:25,912 --> 00:00:32,150
Moreover, abnormalities affecting these steps can lead to infertility or pregnancy loss.

5
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:41,050
The biological and molecular changes involved in human zygote implantation and subsequent fetal and placental development are intricate.

6
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:47,937
In the past 50 years, researchers have delineated many of these molecular and physiological events.

7
00:00:47,937 --> 00:00:53,487
Yet, much work remains in the continual challenge to improve clinical outcomes.

8
00:00:53,487 --> 00:00:56,137
Ovarian-Endometrial Cycle.

9
00:00:56,137 --> 00:01:03,325
In most women, cyclical ovulation continues during the almost 40 years between menarche and menopause.

10
00:01:03,325 --> 00:01:15,762
Thus, without contraception, approximately 400 opportunities for pregnancy exist, and these are tightly regulated by complex interactions of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis.

11
00:01:15,762 --> 00:01:22,612
Concurrently, endometrium undergoes faithfully reproduced cyclical changes to prepare for pregnancy .

12
00:01:22,612 --> 00:01:39,287
Essential contributors in this process include gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), the gonadotropin hormones follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH), and the ovarian sex steroid hormones estrogen and progesterone.

13
00:01:39,287 --> 00:01:48,312
For a detailed description of menstrual cycle physiology, the reader is referred to Chapter 16 in Williams Gynecology, 4th edition .

14
00:01:48,312 --> 00:01:50,587
Figure 5-1.

15
00:01:50,587 --> 00:01:55,175
Gonadotropin control of the ovarian and endometrial cycles.

16
00:01:55,175 --> 00:01:59,975
The ovarian-endometrial cycle is structured as a 28-day cycle.

17
00:01:59,975 --> 00:02:10,787
The follicular phase (days 1 to 14) is characterized by rising estrogen levels, endometrial thickening, and selection of the dominant “ovulatory” follicle.

18
00:02:10,787 --> 00:02:22,075
During the luteal phase (days 14 to 21), the corpus luteum (CL) produces estrogen and progesterone, which prepare the endometrium for implantation.

19
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:34,337
If implantation occurs, the developing blastocyst begins to produce human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) and rescues the corpus luteum, thus maintaining progesterone production.

20
00:02:34,337 --> 00:02:40,250
FSH = follicle-stimulating hormone; LH = luteinizing hormone.

21
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:42,525
# Ovulation.

22
00:02:42,525 --> 00:02:48,125
This defining event separates the follicular and luteal phases of the menstrual cycle.

23
00:02:48,125 --> 00:02:56,437
Following ovulation, the corpus luteum develops from the remains of the graafian follicle in a process referred to as luteinization.

24
00:02:56,437 --> 00:03:08,562
The basement membrane separating the granulosa-lutein and theca-lutein cells breaks down, and by day 2 postovulation, blood vessels and capillaries invade the granulosa cell layer.

25
00:03:08,562 --> 00:03:15,525
During luteinization, these cells undergo hypertrophy and increase their capacity to synthesize hormones.

26
00:03:15,525 --> 00:03:21,262
LH is the primary luteotropic factor responsible for corpus luteum maintenance .

27
00:03:21,262 --> 00:03:26,362
The hormone secretion pattern of the corpus luteum differs from that of the follicle.

28
00:03:26,362 --> 00:03:40,275
First, the greater capacity of granulosa-lutein cells to produce progesterone results from enhanced access to blood-borne, low-density lipoprotein (LDL)-derived cholesterol, which is a steroidogenic precursor .

29
00:03:40,275 --> 00:03:47,350
Ovarian progesterone production peaks at 25 to 50 mg/d during the midluteal phase.

30
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:56,425
With pregnancy, the corpus luteum continues progesterone production in response to placental human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG).

31
00:03:56,425 --> 00:04:01,525
LH and hCG both act via the same LH-hCG receptor.

32
00:04:01,525 --> 00:04:05,550
The human corpus luteum is a transient endocrine organ.

33
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:12,637
In the absence of pregnancy, it rapidly undergoes apoptosis 9 to 11 days after ovulation .

34
00:04:12,637 --> 00:04:20,375
The dramatic drop in circulating estradiol and progesterone levels initiate molecular events that lead to menstruation.

35
00:04:20,375 --> 00:04:33,062
Between days 22 and 25 after ovulation, the secretory-phase endometrium undergoes striking changes associated with predecidual transformation of the upper two thirds of the functionalis layer.

36
00:04:33,062 --> 00:04:38,437
The glands exhibit extensive coiling, and luminal secretions become visible.

37
00:04:38,437 --> 00:04:45,400
Changes within the endometrium can also mark the window of implantation seen on days 20 to 24.

38
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:49,437
Epithelial surface cells show fewer microvilli and

39
00:04:49,588 --> 00:04:54,538
cilia, but luminal protrusions appear on the apical cell surface .

40
00:04:54,488 --> 00:04:58,888
These pinopodes help prepare for blastocyst implantation.

41
00:04:58,888 --> 00:05:05,200
They also coincide with changes in the surface glycocalyx that allow acceptance of a blastocyst .

42
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:11,538
Another highlight of the secretory phase is the continuing growth and development of the spiral arteries.

43
00:05:11,538 --> 00:05:19,838
These vessels arise from the radial arteries, which are myometrial branches of the arcuate and, ultimately, uterine vessels.

44
00:05:19,838 --> 00:05:29,163
The morphological and functional properties of the spiral arteries are unique and essential to blood flow changes seen during menstruation or implantation.

45
00:05:29,163 --> 00:05:37,200
During endometrial growth, spiral arteries lengthen at a rate appreciably greater than the rate of endometrial tissue thickening.

46
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:41,088
This growth discordance obliges even greater coiling.

47
00:05:41,088 --> 00:05:49,575
Spiral artery development reflects a marked induction of angiogenesis, reflected by widespread vessel sprouting and extension.

48
00:05:49,575 --> 00:05:54,038
At this juncture with hormonal withdrawal, menstruation follows.

49
00:05:54,038 --> 00:06:00,163
With blastocyst implantation, however, the endometrium is converted to the decidua.

50
00:06:00,163 --> 00:06:01,838
Decidua.

51
00:06:01,838 --> 00:06:06,688
This is a specialized, highly modified endometrium of pregnancy.

52
00:06:06,688 --> 00:06:13,800
It is essential for hemochorial placentation, that is, one in which maternal blood contacts trophoblast.

53
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:23,863
This relationship requires trophoblast invasion, and considerable research has focused on the interaction between decidual cells and invading trophoblasts.

54
00:06:23,863 --> 00:06:31,000
Decidualization transforms proliferating endometrial stromal cells into specialized secretory cells.

55
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:38,913
This process depends on estrogen, progesterone, androgens, and factors secreted by the implanting blastocyst .

56
00:06:38,913 --> 00:06:49,338
The decidua produces factors that regulate endometrial receptivity and that modulate immune and vascular cell functions within the maternal–fetal microenvironment.

57
00:06:49,338 --> 00:07:02,538
The special immunomodulatory relationship between the decidua and invading trophoblasts is largely mediated by decidual natural killer (NK) cells and ensures success of the pregnancy semiallograft.

58
00:07:02,538 --> 00:07:05,163
# Decidual Structure.

59
00:07:05,163 --> 00:07:10,525
The decidua is classified into three parts based on anatomical location.

60
00:07:10,525 --> 00:07:18,488
Decidua directly beneath the implanted blastocyst is modified by trophoblast invasion and becomes the decidua basalis.

61
00:07:18,488 --> 00:07:26,825
The decidua capsularis overlies the enlarging blastocyst and initially separates the conceptus from the rest of the uterine cavity .

62
00:07:26,825 --> 00:07:36,463
This portion is most prominent during the second month of pregnancy and consists of stromal decidual cells covered by a single layer of flattened epithelial cells.

63
00:07:36,463 --> 00:07:43,875
The other side of the capsularis contacts the avascular, extraembryonic fetal membrane—the chorion laeve.

64
00:07:43,875 --> 00:07:48,288
The remainder of the uterus is lined by decidua parietalis.

65
00:07:48,288 --> 00:07:58,288
During early pregnancy, a space lies between the decidua capsularis and parietalis because the gestational sac does not fill the entire uterine cavity.

66
00:07:58,288 --> 00:08:04,025
The gestational sac is the extraembryonic coelom and also called the chorionic cavity.

67
00:08:04,025 --> 00:08:11,263
By 14 to 16 weeks’ gestation, the expanding sac has enlarged to completely fill the uterine cavity.

68
00:08:11,263 --> 00:08:20,788
The resulting apposition of the decidua capsularis and parietalis creates the decidua vera, and the uterine cavity is functionally obliterated.

69
00:08:20,788 --> 00:08:23,063
Figure 5-2.

70
00:08:23,063 --> 00:08:29,850
Three portions of the decidua—the basalis, capsularis, and parietalis—are illustrated.

71
00:08:29,850 --> 00:08:37,025
In early pregnancy, the decidua begins to thicken, eventually attaining a depth of 5 to 10 mm.

72
00:08:37,025 --> 00:08:44,688
With magnification, furrows and numerous small openings, representing the mouths of uterine glands, can be detected.

73
00:08:44,688 --> 00:08:52,788
Later in pregnancy, the decidua becomes thinner, presumably because of pressure exerted by the expanding uterine contents.

74
00:08:52,788 --> 00:08:57,625
The decidua parietalis and basalis are composed of three layers.

75
00:08:57,625 --> 00:09:10,725
There is a surface or compact zone—zona compacta; a middle portion or spongy zone —zona spongiosa—that has remnants of glands and numerous small blood vessels; and a basal zone—zona basalis.

76
00:09:10,725 --> 00:09:15,700
The zona compacta and spongiosa together form the zona functionalis.

77
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:20,825
The basal zone remains after delivery and gives rise to new endometrium.

78
00:09:20,825 --> 00:09:25,850
The decidual reaction is completed only with blastocyst implantation.

79
00:09:25,850 --> 00:09:28,725
Predecidual changes, however,

80
00:09:28,876 --> 00:09:36,363
commence first during the midluteal phase in endometrial stromal cells adjacent to the spiral arteries and arterioles.

81
00:09:36,313 --> 00:09:41,876
Thereafter, these alterations spread in waves throughout the uterine endometrium.

82
00:09:41,876 --> 00:09:47,638
The endometrial stromal cells enlarge to form polygonal or round decidual cells.

83
00:09:47,638 --> 00:09:56,076
The nuclei become vesicular, and the cytoplasm becomes clear, slightly basophilic, and surrounded by a translucent membrane.

84
00:09:56,076 --> 00:10:01,901
As the embryo–fetus grows, the blood supply to the decidua capsularis is lost.

85
00:10:01,901 --> 00:10:07,201
However, spiral arteries persist to supply the decidua parietalis.

86
00:10:07,201 --> 00:10:14,238
These arteries retain smooth muscle and endothelium and thereby remain responsive to vasoactive agents.

87
00:10:14,238 --> 00:10:23,038
In contrast, the spiral arteries that supply the decidua basalis and ultimately the placental intervillous space are altered remarkably.

88
00:10:23,038 --> 00:10:29,601
Trophoblastic cells invade the spiral arterioles and arteries and replace their endothelial cells.

89
00:10:29,601 --> 00:10:37,788
The vessel wall smooth muscle is destroyed, and the resulting uteroplacental vessels become unresponsive to vasoactive agents.

90
00:10:37,788 --> 00:10:44,726
Defective trophoblastic invasion of spiral arteries is thought to be one underlying cause of preeclampsia .

91
00:10:44,726 --> 00:10:55,138
Conversely, the fetal chorionic vessels, which transport blood between the placenta and the fetus, contain smooth muscle and thus do respond to vasoactive agents.

92
00:10:55,138 --> 00:10:57,938
# Decidual Histology.

93
00:10:57,938 --> 00:11:08,326
Early in pregnancy, the decidual zona spongiosa consists of large distended glands, often exhibiting marked hyperplasia and separated by minimal stroma.

94
00:11:08,326 --> 00:11:17,438
At first, the glands are lined by typical cylindrical uterine epithelium with abundant secretory activity that contributes to blastocyst nourishment.

95
00:11:17,438 --> 00:11:28,401
With advanced pregnancy, the glandular elements largely disappear, and the spongy zone of the decidua basalis consists mainly of arteries and widely dilated veins.

96
00:11:28,401 --> 00:11:33,526
The decidua basalis contributes to formation of the placental basal plate .

97
00:11:33,526 --> 00:11:38,113
As such, it is invaded by many interstitial trophoblasts.

98
00:11:38,113 --> 00:11:45,338
The Nitabuch layer is a zone of fibrinoid degeneration in which invading trophoblasts meet the decidua basalis.

99
00:11:45,338 --> 00:11:51,826
If the decidua is defective, as in placenta accreta, the Nitabuch layer is usually absent .

100
00:11:51,826 --> 00:12:02,663
Normally, there is also a more superficial, but inconsistent, deposition of fibrin—Rohr stria—at the bottom of the intervillous space and surrounding the anchoring villi.

101
00:12:02,663 --> 00:12:08,363
Decidual necrosis is a common phenomenon in the first and probably second trimesters .

102
00:12:08,363 --> 00:12:18,926
Thus, necrotic decidua obtained through curettage after spontaneous abortion should not necessarily be interpreted as either a cause or an effect of the pregnancy loss.

103
00:12:18,926 --> 00:12:21,338
Figure 5-3.

104
00:12:21,338 --> 00:12:28,201
Section through a junction of chorion, villi, and decidua basalis in early first-trimester pregnancy.

105
00:12:28,201 --> 00:12:31,926
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Kurt Benirschke.

106
00:12:31,926 --> 00:12:38,201
Both decidual layers contain numerous cell groups whose composition varies with gestational stage .

107
00:12:38,201 --> 00:12:48,913
The primary cellular components are the true decidual cells, which differentiated from the endometrial stromal cells, and numerous maternal bone marrow-derived cells.

108
00:12:48,913 --> 00:12:59,013
Of the latter, lymphocytes with unique properties accumulate at the maternal–fetal interface and are essential to evoke immune tolerance between mother and fetus.

109
00:12:59,013 --> 00:13:05,638
These include regulatory T cells, decidual macrophages, and decidual NK cells.

110
00:13:05,638 --> 00:13:14,438
Collectively, these cells not only provide immunotolerance but also play an important role in trophoblast invasion and vasculogenesis .

111
00:13:14,438 --> 00:13:17,176
# Decidual Prolactin.

112
00:13:17,176 --> 00:13:27,438
The decidua produces prolactin, which is present in enormous amounts in amnionic fluid (Golander, 1978; Riddick, 1979).

113
00:13:27,438 --> 00:13:38,038
Decidual prolactin is a product of the same gene that encodes for anterior pituitary prolactin, but the exact physiological role of decidual prolactin is unknown.

114
00:13:38,038 --> 00:13:43,313
Signaling by prolactin can lead to the production of proangiogenic factors.

115
00:13:43,313 --> 00:13:52,438
Prolactin can also be cleaved by proteases to form vasoinhibins (Nakajima, 2015; Triebel, 2015).

116
00:13:52,438 --> 00:14:04,901
These compounds have antiangiogenic properties and may contribute to peripartum cardiomyopathy, whose pathogenesis and treatment are discussed in Chapter 52 (p. 933).

117
00:14:04,901 --> 00:14:10,213
Decidual prolactin preferentially enters amnionic fluid and may reach

118
00:14:10,372 --> 00:14:17,584
extraordinarily high levels of 10,000 ng/mL at 20 to 24 weeks’ gestation .

119
00:14:17,534 --> 00:14:25,309
In contrast, maternal serum levels are relatively low at 150 to 200 ng/mL.

120
00:14:25,309 --> 00:14:35,259
As discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 57), prolactin inhibits maternal insulin action and results in increased glucose levels for fetal growth.

121
00:14:35,259 --> 00:14:38,397
Implantation And Early Trophoblast.

122
00:14:38,397 --> 00:14:40,059
Formation.

123
00:14:40,059 --> 00:14:42,497
# Fertilization.

124
00:14:42,497 --> 00:14:50,222
With ovulation, the ovary releases the secondary oocyte and surrounding cells of the cumulus–oocyte complex.

125
00:14:50,222 --> 00:14:55,247
The oocyte complex is quickly engulfed by the fallopian tube infundibulum.

126
00:14:55,247 --> 00:15:01,147
Directional movement of cilia and tubal peristalsis moves the ovum within the tubal lumen.

127
00:15:01,147 --> 00:15:07,597
Fertilization takes place in the lumen within a few hours, and no more than a day after ovulation.

128
00:15:07,597 --> 00:15:14,709
Because of this narrow window, spermatozoa must be present in the fallopian tube at the time of oocyte arrival.

129
00:15:14,709 --> 00:15:21,572
Almost all pregnancies result when intercourse occurs during the 2 days preceding or on the day of ovulation.

130
00:15:21,572 --> 00:15:24,534
Fertilization is highly complex.

131
00:15:24,534 --> 00:15:37,709
Molecular mechanisms allow spermatozoa to pass between cumulus cells; through the zona pellucida, which is a thick glycoprotein layer surrounding the oocyte cell membrane; and into the oocyte cytoplasm.

132
00:15:37,709 --> 00:15:44,122
Fusion of the two nuclei and intermingling of maternal and paternal chromosomes creates the zygote.

133
00:15:44,122 --> 00:15:50,984
Early human development is described by days or weeks postfertilization, that is, postconceptional .

134
00:15:50,984 --> 00:16:00,272
By contrast, in most chapters of this book, clinical pregnancy dating is calculated from the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP).

135
00:16:00,272 --> 00:16:09,197
Thus, 1 week postfertilization corresponds to approximately 3 weeks from the LMP in women with regular 28-day cycles.

136
00:16:09,197 --> 00:16:20,997
This convention is clinically important for dating pregnancies conceived by in vitro fertilization (IVF), in which the gestational age is 14 days greater than the day of fertilization.

137
00:16:20,997 --> 00:16:30,059
As an example, 8 weeks’ gestation refers to 8 completed weeks following the LMP but corresponds to 6 weeks postfertilization.

138
00:16:30,059 --> 00:16:40,359
After fertilization, the zygote—a diploid cell with 46 chromosomes— undergoes cleavage, and zygote cells produced by this division are blastomeres .

139
00:16:40,359 --> 00:16:47,322
In the two-cell zygote, the blastomeres and polar body continue to be surrounded by the zona pellucida.

140
00:16:47,322 --> 00:16:52,572
The zygote undergoes slow cleavage for 3 days while still in the fallopian tube.

141
00:16:52,572 --> 00:16:59,647
As the blastomeres continue to divide, a solid mulberry-like ball of cells—the morula—is produced.

142
00:16:59,647 --> 00:17:05,234
The morula enters the uterine cavity approximately 3 days after fertilization.

143
00:17:05,234 --> 00:17:11,659
Gradual accumulation of fluid between the morula cells leads to formation of the early blastocyst.

144
00:17:11,659 --> 00:17:14,072
Figure 5-4.

145
00:17:14,072 --> 00:17:17,459
Zygote cleavage and blastocyst formation.

146
00:17:17,459 --> 00:17:27,234
The morula period begins at the 12- to 16-cell stage and ends when the blastocyst forms, which occurs when there are 50 to 60 blastomeres present.

147
00:17:27,234 --> 00:17:33,447
The polar bodies, shown in the 2-cell stage, are small nonfunctional cells that degenerate.

148
00:17:33,447 --> 00:17:45,872
Blastocyst As early as 4 to 5 days after fertilization, the 58-cell blastocyst differentiates into five embryo-producing cells—the inner cell mass (see Fig. 5-4).

149
00:17:45,872 --> 00:17:52,259
The remaining 53 outer cells, called the trophectoderm, are destined to form trophoblasts .

150
00:17:52,259 --> 00:18:00,847
In the 107-cell blastocyst, the eight formative, embryo-producing cells are surrounded by 99 trophoblastic cells.

151
00:18:00,847 --> 00:18:12,022
The blastocyst is released from the zona pellucida secondary to secretion of specific proteases from the secretory-phase endometrial glands (O’Sullivan, 2002).

152
00:18:12,022 --> 00:18:20,759
Release from the zona pellucida allows blastocyst-produced cytokines and hormones to directly influence endometrial receptivity .

153
00:18:20,759 --> 00:18:31,184
The blastocyst secretes interleukin-1α (IL-1α) and IL-1β, which are cytokines that likely directly influence the endometrium.

154
00:18:31,184 --> 00:18:40,547
Embryos also secrete hCG, which may influence endometrial receptivity (Licht, 2001; Lobo, 2001).

155
00:18:40,547 --> 00:18:51,409
The receptive endometrium is thought to respond by producing leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF), follistatin, and colony-stimulating factor-1 (CSF-1).

156
00:18:51,409 --> 00:19:03,197
LIF and follistatin activate signaling pathways that collectively inhibit proliferation and promote differentiation of the endometrial epithelia and stroma to enable uterine receptivity .

157
00:19:03,364 --> 00:19:12,714
At the maternal–fetal interface, CSF-1 has proposed immunomodulatory and proangiogenic actions that are required for implantation .

158
00:19:12,664 --> 00:19:15,089
# Implantation.

159
00:19:15,089 --> 00:19:20,889
The blastocyst implants into the uterine wall 6 or 7 days after fertilization.

160
00:19:20,889 --> 00:19:44,614
This process can be divided into three phases: (1) apposition—initial contact of the blastocyst to the uterine wall; (2) adhesion—increased physical contact between the blastocyst and decidua; and (3) invasion— penetration and invasion of syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblasts into the decidua, inner third of the myometrium, and uterine vasculature.

161
00:19:44,614 --> 00:19:53,014
Successful implantation requires a receptive endometrium appropriately primed with estrogen and progesterone by the corpus luteum.

162
00:19:53,014 --> 00:19:59,126
Such uterine receptivity is limited to days 20 to 24 of the endometrial cycle.

163
00:19:59,126 --> 00:20:10,189
Adherence is mediated by cell-surface receptors at the implantation site that interact with blastocyst receptors (Carson, 2002; Lessey, 2002).

164
00:20:10,189 --> 00:20:22,076
If the blastocyst approaches the endometrium after cycle day 24, the potential for adhesion declines because antiadhesive glycoprotein synthesis prevents receptor interactions .

165
00:20:22,076 --> 00:20:30,951
A mismatch between uterine receptivity and timing of embryo transfer can lead to repeated implantation failure in some IVF patients.

166
00:20:30,951 --> 00:20:38,276
This has stimulated efforts to define receptivity by gene expression profiles to improve clinical outcomes .

167
00:20:38,276 --> 00:20:45,676
At the time of its interaction with the endometrium, the blastocyst is composed of 100 to 250 cells.

168
00:20:45,676 --> 00:20:50,676
The blastocyst trophectoderm loosely adheres to the decidua by apposition.

169
00:20:50,676 --> 00:20:59,939
This appears to be closely regulated by paracrine interactions between these two tissues and most commonly occurs on the upper posterior uterine wall.

170
00:20:59,939 --> 00:21:07,826
Successful endometrial blastocyst adhesion involves modified expression of cellular adhesion molecules (CAMs).

171
00:21:07,826 --> 00:21:16,814
The integrins—one of four families of CAMs—are cell-surface receptors that mediate cell adhesion to extracellular matrix proteins .

172
00:21:16,814 --> 00:21:24,301
Endometrial integrins are hormonally regulated, and a specific set of integrins is expressed at implantation .

173
00:21:24,301 --> 00:21:30,439
Recognition-site blockade of the integrins needed for binding will prevent blastocyst attachment .

174
00:21:30,439 --> 00:21:33,314
# Trophoblast Development.

175
00:21:33,314 --> 00:21:38,901
The fetus depends on the placenta for pulmonary, hepatic, and renal functions.

176
00:21:38,901 --> 00:21:45,014
These are accomplished through the anatomical relationship of the placenta and its uterine interface.

177
00:21:45,014 --> 00:21:55,026
In overview, maternal blood flows from uteroplacental vessels into the placental intervillous space and bathes the syncytiotrophoblast, which surround villi.

178
00:21:55,026 --> 00:22:03,514
Here, gases, nutrients, and other substances are exchanged with fetal capillary blood within the core of each placental villus.

179
00:22:03,514 --> 00:22:09,076
Thus, fetal and maternal blood do not normally mix in this hemochorial placenta.

180
00:22:09,076 --> 00:22:17,239
Human placental formation begins with the trophectoderm, which gives rise to a trophoblast cell layer encircling the blastocyst.

181
00:22:17,239 --> 00:22:24,689
Trophoblast exhibits the most variable structure, function, and developmental pattern of all placental components.

182
00:22:24,689 --> 00:22:37,551
Its invasiveness promotes implantation, its nutritional role for the conceptus is reflected in their name, and its endocrine function is essential to maternal physiological adaptations and to pregnancy maintenance.

183
00:22:37,551 --> 00:22:53,676
By the eighth day postfertilization, after initial implantation, the trophoblast has differentiated into an outer multinucleated syncytium—the primitive syncytiotrophoblast, and an inner layer of primitive mononuclear cells—cytotrophoblasts.

184
00:22:53,676 --> 00:22:57,514
The latter are solely germinal cells for the syncytium.

185
00:22:57,514 --> 00:23:08,864
As cytotrophoblasts differentiate, they express an endogenous envelope protein called syncytin, which aids their cell fusion with the expanding outer layer of syncytiotrophoblast.

186
00:23:08,864 --> 00:23:17,726
Each cytotrophoblast has a well-demarcated cell border, a single nucleus, and ability to undergo DNA synthesis and mitosis .

187
00:23:17,726 --> 00:23:24,014
These are lacking in the syncytiotrophoblast, which provides transport functions of the placenta.

188
00:23:24,014 --> 00:23:35,901
It is so named because instead of individual cells, it has an amorphous cytoplasm without cell borders, nuclei that are multiple and diverse in size and shape, and a continuous syncytial lining.

189
00:23:35,901 --> 00:23:37,489
Following

190
00:23:37,660 --> 00:23:46,072
implantation, trophoblasts differentiate along two main pathways that give rise to either villous or extravillous trophoblasts.

191
00:23:46,022 --> 00:23:50,822
As shown in Figure 5-5, both have distinct functions .

192
00:23:50,822 --> 00:24:00,235
Villous trophoblasts generate chorionic villi, which primarily transport oxygen, nutrients, and other compounds between the fetus and mother.

193
00:24:00,235 --> 00:24:05,135
Extravillous trophoblasts migrate into the decidua and myometrium .

194
00:24:05,135 --> 00:24:11,672
They also penetrate maternal vasculature and thus directly contact various maternal cell types .

195
00:24:11,672 --> 00:24:18,985
Extravillous trophoblasts are further classified as interstitial trophoblasts and endovascular trophoblasts.

196
00:24:18,985 --> 00:24:27,072
The interstitial trophoblasts invade the decidua and eventually penetrate the myometrium to form placental-bed giant cells.

197
00:24:27,072 --> 00:24:31,022
These trophoblasts also surround spiral arteries.

198
00:24:31,022 --> 00:24:35,960
The endovascular trophoblasts penetrate the spiral artery lumens .

199
00:24:35,960 --> 00:24:38,485
Figure 5-5.

200
00:24:38,485 --> 00:24:44,297
Endovascular and interstitial extravillous trophoblasts are found outside the villus.

201
00:24:44,297 --> 00:24:53,910
Endovascular trophoblasts invade and transform spiral arteries during pregnancy to create lowresistance blood flow that is characteristic of the placenta.

202
00:24:53,910 --> 00:24:59,122
Interstitial trophoblasts invade the decidua and surround spiral arteries.

203
00:24:59,122 --> 00:25:01,597
Figure 5-6.

204
00:25:01,597 --> 00:25:07,097
Photomicrograph of extravillous trophoblast invasion of the decidua basalis.

205
00:25:07,097 --> 00:25:18,897
Recently, single-cell RNA sequencing technology has enabled inference of trophoblast differentiation lineages (Liu, 2018; Tsang, 2017).

206
00:25:18,897 --> 00:25:26,885
This has also yielded insights into the communication between trophoblasts and maternal decidual and immune cell populations .

207
00:25:26,885 --> 00:25:35,097
Furthermore, human trophoblast stem cells, long hypothesized to exist, have been isolated from early gestations .

208
00:25:35,097 --> 00:25:45,897
Culture of these cells will likely aid greater understanding of early trophoblast differentiation and invasion (Haider, 2018; Turco, 2018).

209
00:25:45,897 --> 00:25:48,372
# Early Invasion.

210
00:25:48,372 --> 00:25:55,735
After gentle erosion between epithelial cells of the surface endometrium, invading trophoblasts burrow deeper.

211
00:25:55,735 --> 00:26:02,772
At 9 days of development, the blastocyst wall facing the uterine lumen is a single layer of flattened cells.

212
00:26:02,772 --> 00:26:08,322
By the 10th day, the blastocyst becomes totally encased within the endometrium .

213
00:26:08,322 --> 00:26:17,147
The blastocyst wall opposite the uterine lumen is thicker and comprises two zones—the trophoblasts and the embryo-forming inner cell mass.

214
00:26:17,147 --> 00:26:28,647
As early as 7.5 days postfertilization, the inner cell mass or embryonic disc differentiates into a thick plate of primitive ectoderm and an underlying layer of endoderm.

215
00:26:28,647 --> 00:26:36,460
Some small cells appear between the embryonic disc and the trophoblasts and enclose a space that will become the amnionic cavity.

216
00:26:36,460 --> 00:26:38,910
Figure 5-7.

217
00:26:38,910 --> 00:26:42,572
Drawing of sections through implanted blastocysts.

218
00:26:42,572 --> 00:26:44,947
A. At 10 days.

219
00:26:44,947 --> 00:26:48,297
B. At 12 days after fertilization.

220
00:26:48,297 --> 00:26:54,210
This stage is characterized by the intercommunication of the lacunae filled with maternal blood.

221
00:26:54,210 --> 00:27:02,572
Note in (B) that large cavities have appeared in the extraembryonic mesoderm, forming the beginning of the extraembryonic coelom.

222
00:27:02,572 --> 00:27:09,210
Also, extraembryonic endodermal cells have begun to form on the inside of the primitive yolk sac.

223
00:27:09,210 --> 00:27:16,360
Redrawn from Moore KL, Persaud, TV, Torchia, MG (eds): The Developing Human.

224
00:27:16,360 --> 00:27:23,797
Clinically Oriented Embryology, 9th ed. Philadelphia, PA: Saunders; 2013.

225
00:27:23,797 --> 00:27:33,597
Extraembryonic mesenchyme first appears as groups of isolated cells within the blastocyst cavity, and later this mesoderm completely lines the cavity.

226
00:27:33,597 --> 00:27:41,572
Within this mesoderm, spaces form and then fuse to form the chorionic cavity, that is, the extraembryonic coelom.

227
00:27:41,572 --> 00:27:45,547
The chorion is composed of trophoblasts and mesenchyme.

228
00:27:45,547 --> 00:27:50,247
Some mesenchymal cells eventually will condense to form the body stalk.

229
00:27:50,247 --> 00:27:56,422
This stalk joins the embryo to the nutrient chorion and later develops into the umbilical cord.

230
00:27:56,422 --> 00:28:05,347
The body stalk can be recognized at an early stage at the caudal end of the embryonic disc (Fig. 7-3, p. 123).

231
00:28:05,347 --> 00:28:12,022
As the embryo enlarges, more maternal decidua basalis is invaded by syncytiotrophoblast.

232
00:28:12,022 --> 00:28:22,235
Beginning approximately 12 days after conception, the syncytiotrophoblast is permeated by a system of intercommunicating channels called trophoblastic lacunae.

233
00:28:22,235 --> 00:28:28,947
After invasion of superficial decidual capillary walls, lacunae become filled with maternal blood.

234
00:28:28,947 --> 00:28:30,810
At the same time,

235
00:28:30,964 --> 00:28:39,889
the decidual reaction, characterized by decidual stromal cell enlargement and glycogen storage, intensifies (p. 84).

236
00:28:39,839 --> 00:28:42,489
# Chorionic Villi.

237
00:28:42,489 --> 00:28:53,589
With deeper invasion into the decidua, solid primary villi arise from buds of cytotrophoblasts that protrude into the primitive syncytium before 12 days postfertilization.

238
00:28:53,589 --> 00:28:59,664
Primary villi are composed of a cytotrophoblast core covered by syncytiotrophoblast.

239
00:28:59,664 --> 00:29:07,251
As the lacunae merge, a complicated labyrinth is formed that is partitioned by these solid cytotrophoblastic columns.

240
00:29:07,251 --> 00:29:15,064
The trophoblast-lined channels form the intervillous space, and the solid cellular columns form the primary villous stalks.

241
00:29:15,064 --> 00:29:24,076
Beginning on the 12th day after fertilization, mesenchymal cords derived from extraembryonic mesoderm invade the solid trophoblast columns.

242
00:29:24,076 --> 00:29:27,039
These then form secondary villi.

243
00:29:27,039 --> 00:29:32,876
Once angiogenesis begins in the mesenchymal cords, tertiary villi are created.

244
00:29:32,876 --> 00:29:42,414
Although maternal venous sinuses are tapped early in implantation, maternal arterial blood does not enter the intervillous space until around day 15.

245
00:29:42,414 --> 00:29:49,739
By the 17th day, however, fetal blood vessels are functional, and a placental circulation is established.

246
00:29:49,739 --> 00:29:56,914
The fetal– placental circulation is completed when the blood vessels of the embryo are connected with chorionic vessels.

247
00:29:56,914 --> 00:30:01,564
In some villi, angiogenesis fails from lack of circulation.

248
00:30:01,564 --> 00:30:09,389
The most striking exaggeration of this is seen with hydatidiform mole (Fig. 13-1, p. 236).

249
00:30:09,389 --> 00:30:15,939
Villi are covered by an outer layer of syncytiotrophoblast and an inner layer of cytotrophoblasts.

250
00:30:15,939 --> 00:30:23,601
Proliferation of cytotrophoblast at the villous tips produces the trophoblastic cell columns that form anchoring villi.

251
00:30:23,601 --> 00:30:32,226
They are not invaded by fetal mesenchyme, and they are anchored to the decidua at the basal plate, which is the maternal side of the intervillous space.

252
00:30:32,226 --> 00:30:36,376
The chorionic plate forms the roof of the intervillous space.

253
00:30:36,376 --> 00:30:44,164
It consists of the two layers of trophoblasts, which line the intervillous space, and fibrous mesoderm on the opposite side.

254
00:30:44,164 --> 00:30:52,264
The final chorionic plate is formed by 8 to 10 weeks as the amnionic and primary chorionic plate mesenchyme fuse together.

255
00:30:52,264 --> 00:30:56,989
This formation is accomplished by expansion of the amnionic sac.

256
00:30:56,989 --> 00:31:03,264
Early electron microscopic studies demonstrate prominent microvilli on the syncytial surface .

257
00:31:03,264 --> 00:31:10,714
Associated pinocytotic vacuoles and vesicles are involved in both absorptive and secretory placental functions.

258
00:31:10,714 --> 00:31:19,939
Microvilli increase trophoblast surface area in direct contact with maternal blood, the defining characteristic of a hemochorial placenta.

259
00:31:19,939 --> 00:31:22,126
Figure 5-8.

260
00:31:22,126 --> 00:31:26,051
Electron micrograph of term human placenta villus.

261
00:31:26,051 --> 00:31:33,939
A villus capillary filled with fetal red blood cells (asterisks) is seen in close proximity to the microvilli border.

262
00:31:33,939 --> 00:31:40,089
Reproduced with permission from Boyd JD, Hamilton WJ: The Human Placenta.

263
00:31:40,089 --> 00:31:43,426
Cambridge, Heffer, 1970.

264
00:31:43,426 --> 00:31:45,701
Placenta And Chorion.

265
00:31:45,701 --> 00:31:48,364
# Chorion Development.

266
00:31:48,364 --> 00:31:54,876
In early pregnancy, the villi are distributed over the entire periphery of the chorionic membrane .

267
00:31:54,876 --> 00:32:03,189
As the blastocyst with its surrounding trophoblasts grows and expands into the decidua, one pole faces the endometrial cavity.

268
00:32:03,189 --> 00:32:06,276
The opposite pole will form the placenta.

269
00:32:06,276 --> 00:32:14,401
Here, chorionic villi in contact with the decidua basalis proliferate to form the chorion frondosum—or leafy chorion.

270
00:32:14,401 --> 00:32:23,264
As growth of embryonic and extraembryonic tissues continues, the blood supply to the chorion facing the endometrial cavity is restricted.

271
00:32:23,264 --> 00:32:29,651
Because of this, villi in contact with the decidua capsularis cease to grow and then degenerate.

272
00:32:29,651 --> 00:32:38,951
This portion of the chorion becomes the avascular fetal membrane that abuts the decidua parietalis and is called the chorion laeve—or smooth chorion.

273
00:32:38,951 --> 00:32:44,751
This smooth chorion is composed of cytotrophoblasts and fetal mesodermal mesenchyme.

274
00:32:44,751 --> 00:32:50,126
A paracrine system between the decidua and chorion also links mother and fetus.

275
00:32:50,126 --> 00:32:58,751
This is an extraordinarily important arrangement for maternal–fetal communication and for maternal immunological acceptance of the conceptus .

276
00:32:58,751 --> 00:33:01,151
Figure 5-9.

277
00:33:01,151 --> 00:33:03,751
Complete abortion specimens.

278
00:33:03,751 --> 00:33:12,326
A. Initially, the entire chorionic sac is covered with villi, and the embryo within is not visible B. Stretch and pressure from

279
00:33:12,484 --> 00:33:16,096
further growth prompt partial regression of the villi.

280
00:33:16,046 --> 00:33:21,721
The remaining villi form the future placenta, whereas the smooth portion is the chorion.

281
00:33:21,721 --> 00:33:28,696
Until near the end of the third month, the chorion laeve is separated from the amnion by the exocoelomic cavity.

282
00:33:28,696 --> 00:33:34,271
Thereafter, they are in intimate contact to form the avascular amniochorion.

283
00:33:34,271 --> 00:33:40,009
These two structures are important sites of molecular transfer and metabolic activity.

284
00:33:40,009 --> 00:33:46,596
Moreover, they constitute an important paracrine arm of the fetal–maternal communication system.

285
00:33:46,596 --> 00:33:50,246
# Regulators of Trophoblast Invasion.

286
00:33:50,246 --> 00:34:06,096
Implantation and endometrial decidualization activate a unique population of maternal immune cells that play critical functions in trophoblast invasion, angiogenesis, spiral artery remodeling, and maternal tolerance to fetal alloantigens.

287
00:34:06,096 --> 00:34:15,971
Decidual natural killer cells (dNK) make up 70 percent of decidual leukocytes in the first trimester and directly contact trophoblasts.

288
00:34:15,971 --> 00:34:22,646
In contrast to NK cells in peripheral blood, dNK cells lack cytotoxic functions.

289
00:34:22,646 --> 00:34:30,771
They produce specific cytokines and angiogenic factors to regulate trophoblast invasion and spiral artery remodeling .

290
00:34:30,771 --> 00:34:34,871
dNK cells promote phagocytosis of cell debris .

291
00:34:34,871 --> 00:34:47,759
These and other unique properties distinguish dNK cells from circulating NK cells and from NK cells in the endometrium before pregnancy (Fu, 2013; Winger, 2013).

292
00:34:47,759 --> 00:34:59,946
dNK cells express both IL-8 and interferon-inducible protein 10, which bind to receptors on invasive trophoblastic cells to promote their decidual invasion toward the spiral arteries.

293
00:34:59,946 --> 00:35:11,046
dNK cells also produce proangiogenic factors, including VEGF and placental growth factor (PlGF), which both promote vascular growth in the decidua.

294
00:35:11,046 --> 00:35:18,271
Trophoblasts also secrete specific chemokines that attract the dNK cells to the maternal–fetal interface.

295
00:35:18,271 --> 00:35:22,484
Thus, both cell types simultaneously attract each other.

296
00:35:22,484 --> 00:35:32,221
Decidual macrophages account for approximately 20 percent of leukocytes in the first trimester and elicit an M2-immunomodulatory phenotype .

297
00:35:32,221 --> 00:35:41,371
Recall that while M1 macrophages are proinflammatory, M2 macrophages counter proinflammatory responses and promote tissue repair.

298
00:35:41,371 --> 00:35:46,659
Concurrently, T cell subsets aid tolerance toward the allogenic fetus.

299
00:35:46,659 --> 00:35:52,271
Regulatory T cells (Tregs) are essential for promoting immune tolerance.

300
00:35:52,271 --> 00:36:01,371
Other T cell subsets are present, such as Th1, Th2, and Th17, although their functions are tightly regulated .

301
00:36:01,371 --> 00:36:07,996
Endometrial Invasion Extravillous trophoblasts of the first-trimester placenta are highly invasive.

302
00:36:07,996 --> 00:36:16,734
This process occurs under low-oxygen conditions, and regulatory factors that are induced under hypoxic conditions are contributory .

303
00:36:16,734 --> 00:36:26,559
Invasive trophoblasts secrete numerous proteolytic enzymes that digest extracellular matrix and activate proteinases already present in the decidua.

304
00:36:26,559 --> 00:36:35,971
Trophoblasts produce urokinase-type plasminogen activator, which converts plasminogen into the broadly acting serine protease, plasmin.

305
00:36:35,971 --> 00:36:41,171
This in turn both degrades matrix proteins and activates MMPs.

306
00:36:41,171 --> 00:36:46,446
One member of the MMP family, MMP-9, appears to be critical.

307
00:36:46,446 --> 00:36:54,434
The timing and extent of trophoblast invasion is regulated by a balanced interplay between pro- and anti-invasive factors.

308
00:36:54,434 --> 00:37:01,884
Low estradiol levels in the first trimester are critical for trophoblast invasion and spiral artery remodeling.

309
00:37:01,884 --> 00:37:13,509
Animal studies suggest that the rise in second-trimester estradiol levels suppresses and limits vessel remodeling by reducing trophoblast expression of VEGF and specific integrin receptors .

310
00:37:13,509 --> 00:37:23,946
Namely, extravillous trophoblasts express integrin receptors that recognize the extracellular matrix proteins collagen IV, laminin, and fibronectin.

311
00:37:23,946 --> 00:37:32,484
Binding of these matrix proteins and integrin receptors initiates signals to promote trophoblast cell migration and differentiation.

312
00:37:32,484 --> 00:37:40,259
However, as pregnancy advances, rising estradiol levels downregulate VEGF and integrin receptor expression.

313
00:37:40,259 --> 00:37:45,096
This represses and controls the extent of uterine vessel transformation.

314
00:37:45,096 --> 00:37:48,071
# Spiral Artery Invasion.

315
00:37:48,071 --> 00:37:57,996
One of the most remarkable features of human placental development is the extensive modification of maternal vasculature by trophoblasts, which are fetal in origin.

316
00:37:57,996 --> 00:37:59,546
These

317
00:37:59,716 --> 00:38:05,491
events occur in the first half of pregnancy and are essential to uteroplacental blood flow.

318
00:38:05,441 --> 00:38:12,278
They are also integral to conditions such as preeclampsia, fetal-growth restriction, and preterm birth.

319
00:38:12,278 --> 00:38:28,203
Spiral artery modifications are carried out by two populations of extravillous trophoblasts—endovascular trophoblasts, which penetrate the spiral-artery lumen, and interstitial trophoblasts, which surround the arteries (see Fig. 55).

320
00:38:28,203 --> 00:38:33,228
Interstitial trophoblasts constitute a major portion of the placental bed.

321
00:38:33,228 --> 00:38:42,453
They penetrate the decidua and adjacent myometrium and aggregate around spiral arteries, where they may aid endovascular trophoblast invasion.

322
00:38:42,453 --> 00:38:49,278
Endovascular trophoblasts first enter the spiral artery lumens and initially form cellular plugs.

323
00:38:49,278 --> 00:38:56,203
They then promote apoptosis of the native endothelium and replace the vessel lumen with cells of fetal origin.

324
00:38:56,203 --> 00:39:01,878
Fibrinoid material replaces smooth muscle and connective tissue of the vessel media.

325
00:39:01,878 --> 00:39:06,391
This decreases resistance of blood flow into the intervillous space.

326
00:39:06,391 --> 00:39:15,228
Invading endovascular trophoblasts can extend several centimeters along the vessel lumen, and they must migrate against arterial flow.

327
00:39:15,228 --> 00:39:22,516
Of note, invasion by trophoblasts involves only the decidual spiral arteries and not decidual veins.

328
00:39:22,516 --> 00:39:27,403
Uteroplacental vessel development proceeds in two waves or stages .

329
00:39:27,403 --> 00:39:36,528
First, before 12 weeks’ postfertilization, spiral arteries are invaded and modified up to the border between the decidua and myometrium.

330
00:39:36,528 --> 00:39:45,066
The second wave, between 12 and 16 weeks, involves some invasion of the intramyometrial segments of spiral arteries.

331
00:39:45,066 --> 00:39:52,816
Remodeling converts narrowlumen, muscular spiral arteries into dilated, low-resistance uteroplacental vessels.

332
00:39:52,816 --> 00:40:05,316
Molecular mechanisms of these crucial events and their significance for preeclampsia and fetal-growth restriction have been reviewed (Pereira de Sousa, 2017; Xie, 2016).

333
00:40:05,316 --> 00:40:20,891
Although early placenta development occurs in a low-oxygen-tension environment (2 to 3 percent O2), this vascular remodeling increases blood flow and oxygenation such that the oxygen concentration more than doubles around the end of the first trimester .

334
00:40:20,891 --> 00:40:23,353
# Villus Branching.

335
00:40:23,353 --> 00:40:34,641
Although certain villi of the chorion frondosum extend from the chorionic plate to the decidua to serve as anchoring villi, most villi arborize and end freely within the intervillous space.

336
00:40:34,641 --> 00:40:44,741
As gestation proceeds, the short, thick, early stem villi branch to form progressively finer subdivisions and greater numbers of increasingly smaller villi .

337
00:40:44,741 --> 00:40:50,766
This increasing villous surface area correlates with gestational age and aids fetal growth.

338
00:40:50,766 --> 00:40:58,878
In cases of restricted blood flow, more highly branched villi than expected for gestational age is a compensatory mechanism.

339
00:40:58,878 --> 00:41:01,191
Figure 5-10.

340
00:41:01,191 --> 00:41:13,178
Illustration (A, B), histology (C, D), and electron microscopy (E, F) of early (top panel) and late (bottom panel) human placenta villi.

341
00:41:13,178 --> 00:41:17,116
Limited branching of villi is seen in the early placenta.

342
00:41:17,116 --> 00:41:25,266
With maturation, increasing villous arborization is seen, and villous capillaries lie closer to the surface of each villus.

343
00:41:25,266 --> 00:41:35,978
Electron micrographs reproduced with permission from King BF, Menton DN: Scanning electron microscopy of human placental villi from early and late in gestation.

344
00:41:35,978 --> 00:41:42,153
Am J Obstet Gynecol 122:824, 1975.

345
00:41:42,153 --> 00:41:48,866
Each truncal or main stem villus and its ramifications constitutes a placental lobule or cotyledon.

346
00:41:48,866 --> 00:41:57,078
Each lobule has a single chorionic artery and vein, such that lobules constitute the functional units of placental architecture.

347
00:41:57,078 --> 00:42:00,391
# Placental Growth and Maturation.

348
00:42:00,391 --> 00:42:05,666
In the first trimester, placental growth is more rapid than that of the fetus.

349
00:42:05,666 --> 00:42:11,578
However, by 17 weeks’ gestation, placental and fetal weights are nearly equal.

350
00:42:11,578 --> 00:42:16,316
By term, placental weight approximates one sixth of fetal weight.

351
00:42:16,316 --> 00:42:23,041
The mature placenta and its variant forms are discussed in detail in Chapter 6 (p. 108).

352
00:42:23,041 --> 00:42:33,741
Briefly, viewed from the maternal surface, which attaches to the uterine wall, the number of slightly elevated convex areas, called lobes, varies from 10 to 38.

353
00:42:33,741 --> 00:42:38,103
Lobes are incompletely separated by grooves of variable depth.

354
00:42:38,103 --> 00:42:42,753
These tissue grooves form in response to placental septa, which

355
00:42:42,916 --> 00:42:46,028
rise up as projections of decidua.

356
00:42:45,978 --> 00:42:55,791
The total number of placental lobes remains the same throughout gestation, and individual lobes continue to grow—although less actively in late pregnancy .

357
00:42:55,791 --> 00:43:01,653
Although grossly visible lobes are commonly referred to as cotyledons, this is inaccurate.

358
00:43:01,653 --> 00:43:08,503
Correctly used, lobules or cotyledons are the functional units supplied by each main stem villus.

359
00:43:08,503 --> 00:43:16,916
As villi continue to branch and the terminal ramifications become more numerous and smaller, the volume of cytotrophoblasts decline.

360
00:43:16,916 --> 00:43:24,616
This layer attenuates, and by 16 weeks’ gestation, the apparent continuity of the cytotrophoblasts is lost.

361
00:43:24,616 --> 00:43:34,253
As the syncytium also thins, the fetal vessels become more prominent within the villus and lie closer to the intervillous space (see Fig. 5-8).

362
00:43:34,253 --> 00:43:45,491
At term, villi may be focally reduced to a thin layer of syncytium covering minimal villous connective tissue in which thin-walled fetal capillaries abut the trophoblast and dominate the villi.

363
00:43:45,491 --> 00:43:50,216
The villous stroma also exhibits changes as gestation progresses.

364
00:43:50,216 --> 00:43:57,591
In early pregnancy, the branching connective-tissue cells are separated by an abundant loose intercellular matrix.

365
00:43:57,591 --> 00:44:03,641
Later, the villous stroma becomes denser, and the cells are more spindly and closely packed.

366
00:44:03,641 --> 00:44:10,016
Another stromal change involves infiltration of Hofbauer cells, which are fetal macrophages.

367
00:44:10,016 --> 00:44:17,266
These are round with vesicular, often eccentric nuclei and with very granular or vacuolated cytoplasm.

368
00:44:17,266 --> 00:44:25,953
They grow in number and maturational state throughout pregnancy and appear to be important mediators of protection at the maternal–fetal interface .

369
00:44:25,953 --> 00:44:40,778
These macrophages are phagocytic, have an immunosuppressive phenotype, produce various cytokines, and provide paracrine regulation of trophoblastic functions (Cervar, 1999; Reyes, 2017).

370
00:44:40,778 --> 00:44:46,953
Some architectural changes, if substantive, can lower placental exchange efficiency.

371
00:44:46,953 --> 00:44:58,503
These include thickening of the basal lamina of trophoblast or capillaries, obliteration of certain fetal vessels, greater villous stroma, and fibrin deposition on the villous surface .

372
00:44:58,503 --> 00:45:01,341
# Placental Circulation.

373
00:45:01,341 --> 00:45:08,428
The gross anatomy of the placenta reflects the intimate approximation of the fetal capillary bed to maternal blood.

374
00:45:08,428 --> 00:45:14,753
The fetal surface is covered by the transparent amnion, beneath which chorionic vessels course.

375
00:45:14,753 --> 00:45:27,453
A section through the placenta includes amnion, chorion, chorionic villi and intervillous space, decidual (basal) plate, and myometrium (Figs. 5-11 and 5-12).

376
00:45:27,453 --> 00:45:29,828
Figure 5-11.

377
00:45:29,828 --> 00:45:34,203
Uterus showing a normal placenta and its membranes in situ.

378
00:45:34,203 --> 00:45:36,603
Figure 5-12.

379
00:45:36,603 --> 00:45:42,016
Photomicrograph of implantation site with partial section of an early embryo.

380
00:45:42,016 --> 00:45:47,816
Anchoring villi are seen with extravillous trophoblasts invading the decidua basalis.

381
00:45:47,816 --> 00:45:51,016
CNS = central nervous system.

382
00:45:51,016 --> 00:45:54,741
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Kurt Benirschke.

383
00:45:54,741 --> 00:46:02,003
Fetal Circulation Deoxygenated venous-like fetal blood flows to the placenta through the two umbilical arteries.

384
00:46:02,003 --> 00:46:10,241
As the cord joins the placenta, these umbilical vessels branch repeatedly beneath the amnion as they run across the chorionic plate.

385
00:46:10,241 --> 00:46:16,991
Branching continues within the villi to ultimately form capillary networks in the terminal villous branches.

386
00:46:16,991 --> 00:46:24,428
Blood with significantly higher oxygen content returns from the placenta via a single umbilical vein to the fetus.

387
00:46:24,428 --> 00:46:32,666
The branches of the umbilical vessels that traverse along the chorionic plate are called placental surface vessels or chorionic vessels.

388
00:46:32,666 --> 00:46:41,028
They respond to vasoactive substances, but anatomically, morphologically, histologically, and functionally, they are unique.

389
00:46:41,028 --> 00:46:45,328
Chorionic arteries always cross over chorionic veins.

390
00:46:45,328 --> 00:46:53,803
Vessels are most readily recognized by this anatomical relationship, but they are difficult to distinguish by histological criteria.

391
00:46:53,803 --> 00:46:59,916
Truncal arteries are perforating branches of the surface arteries and pass through the chorionic plate.

392
00:46:59,916 --> 00:47:05,203
Each truncal artery supplies one main stem villus and thus one cotyledon.

393
00:47:05,203 --> 00:47:12,191
As the artery penetrates the chorionic plate, its wall loses smooth muscle, and its caliber increases.

394
00:47:12,191 --> 00:47:18,028
The loss of muscle continues as the truncal arteries and veins branch into their smaller rami.

395
00:47:18,028 --> 00:47:20,091
Before 10 weeks’

396
00:47:20,260 --> 00:47:31,385
gestation, end-diastolic flow is not detected within the umbilical artery at the end of the fetal cardiac cycle (Fisk, 1988; Loquet, 1988).

397
00:47:31,335 --> 00:47:38,210
After 10 weeks, however, end-diastolic flow appears and is maintained throughout normal pregnancy.

398
00:47:38,210 --> 00:47:44,260
Clinically, these flow patterns are studied with Doppler sonography to assess fetal well-being .

399
00:47:44,260 --> 00:48:02,947
Maternal Circulation Mechanisms of placental blood flow must allow blood to leave maternal circulation; flow into an amorphous space lined by syncytiotrophoblast; and return through maternal veins without producing arteriovenous-like shunts that would prevent adequate exchange between maternal blood and fetal villi.

400
00:48:02,947 --> 00:48:12,085
For this, maternal blood enters through the basal plate and is driven high up toward the chorionic plate by arterial pressure before laterally dispersing .

401
00:48:12,085 --> 00:48:20,935
After bathing the external microvillous surface, maternal blood drains back through venous orifices in the basal plate and enters uterine veins.

402
00:48:20,935 --> 00:48:26,472
Thus, maternal blood traverses the placenta randomly without preformed channels.

403
00:48:26,472 --> 00:48:35,510
Trophoblast invasion of the spiral arteries creates low-resistance vessels that can accommodate massive increase in uterine perfusion during gestation.

404
00:48:35,510 --> 00:48:41,985
Generally, spiral arteries are perpendicular to, but veins are parallel to, the uterine wall.

405
00:48:41,985 --> 00:48:50,072
This arrangement aids closure of veins during a uterine contraction and prevents the exit of maternal blood from the intervillous space.

406
00:48:50,072 --> 00:49:00,210
The number of arterial openings into the intervillous space is gradually reduced by cytotrophoblastic invasion to approximately 120 entry sites at term .

407
00:49:00,210 --> 00:49:04,422
These discharge blood in spurts to bath the adjacent villi .

408
00:49:04,422 --> 00:49:15,385
After the 30th week, a prominent venous plexus lies between the decidua basalis and myometrium and helps develop the cleavage plane needed for placental separation after delivery.

409
00:49:15,385 --> 00:49:18,085
Figure 5-13.

410
00:49:18,085 --> 00:49:22,135
Schematic drawing of a section through a full-term placenta.

411
00:49:22,135 --> 00:49:31,472
Maternal blood flows into the intervillous spaces in funnel-shaped spurts, and umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated fetal blood to the placenta.

412
00:49:31,472 --> 00:49:37,622
Exchanges between the maternal and fetal systems occur as maternal blood flows around the villi.

413
00:49:37,622 --> 00:49:42,210
The umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood back to the fetus.

414
00:49:42,210 --> 00:49:51,885
Inflowing arterial blood pushes maternal venous blood into the endometrial veins, which are scattered over the entire surface of the decidua basalis.

415
00:49:51,885 --> 00:49:57,360
Placental lobes are separated from each other by placental (decidual) septa.

416
00:49:57,360 --> 00:50:01,972
Both inflow and outflow are curtailed during uterine contractions.

417
00:50:01,972 --> 00:50:13,172
Bleker and associates (1975) used serial sonography during normal labor and found that placental length, thickness, and surface area grew during contractions.

418
00:50:13,172 --> 00:50:20,485
They attributed this to distention of the intervillous space by impairment of venous outflow compared with arterial inflow.

419
00:50:20,485 --> 00:50:28,610
During contractions, therefore, a somewhat larger volume of blood is available for exchange even though the rate of flow is decreased.

420
00:50:28,610 --> 00:50:37,522
Similarly, Doppler velocimetry has shown that diastolic flow velocity in spiral arteries is diminished during uterine contractions.

421
00:50:37,522 --> 00:50:50,335
Thus, principal factors regulating intervillous space blood flow are arterial blood pressure, intrauterine pressure, uterine contraction pattern, and factors that act specifically on arterial walls.

422
00:50:50,335 --> 00:50:53,360
# Breaks in the Placental “Barrier”.

423
00:50:53,360 --> 00:51:02,947
The placenta does not maintain absolute integrity of the fetal and maternal circulations, and cells traffic between mother and fetus in both directions.

424
00:51:02,947 --> 00:51:09,172
This situation is best exemplified clinically by erythrocyte D-antigen alloimmunization .

425
00:51:09,172 --> 00:51:16,710
Fetal cell transfer is small in most cases, although rarely the fetus exsanguinates into the maternal circulation.

426
00:51:16,710 --> 00:51:22,847
Fetal cells can also engraft in the mother during pregnancy and still be detected decades later.

427
00:51:22,847 --> 00:51:38,960
Fetal lymphocytes, mesenchymal stem cells, and endothelial colony-forming cells all reside in maternal blood, bone marrow, or uterine vasculature (Huu, 2006; Piper, 2007; Sipos, 2013).

428
00:51:38,960 --> 00:51:51,172
This frequent phenomenon, termed microchimerism, is implicated in the disparate female: male ratio of autoimmune disorders (Greer, 2011; Stevens, 2006).

429
00:51:51,172 --> 00:51:53,460
As discussed in Chapter

430
00:51:53,620 --> 00:52:05,720
62 (p. 1109), fetal cell engraftment has been associated with the pathogenesis of lymphocytic thyroiditis, scleroderma, and systemic lupus erythematosus.

431
00:52:05,670 --> 00:52:08,870
# Maternal–Fetal Interface.

432
00:52:08,870 --> 00:52:19,645
This maternal–fetal interface is an active hub of immunological interactions that allows implantation, appropriate placental development, and immunotolerance of the fetus.

433
00:52:19,645 --> 00:52:25,007
At the same time, a functional immune system must be maintained to protect the mother.

434
00:52:25,007 --> 00:52:33,882
Immunogenicity of the Trophoblasts Trophoblastic cells are the only fetus-derived cells in direct contact with maternal tissues and blood.

435
00:52:33,882 --> 00:52:44,482
Fetal syncytiotrophoblast synthesizes and secretes numerous factors that regulate the immune responses of maternal cells both at the implantation site and systemically.

436
00:52:44,482 --> 00:52:52,432
Human leukocyte antigens (HLAs) are the human analogue of the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) .

437
00:52:52,432 --> 00:53:04,695
There are 17 HLA class I genes and include three classic genes, HLA-A, -B, and -C, that encode the major class I (class Ia) transplantation antigens.

438
00:53:04,695 --> 00:53:12,782
Three other class I genes, designated HLA-E, -F, and -G, encode class Ib HLA antigens.

439
00:53:12,782 --> 00:53:21,845
MHC class I and II antigens are absent from villous trophoblasts, which appear to be immunologically inert at all gestational stages .

440
00:53:21,845 --> 00:53:29,907
Invasive extravillous trophoblasts do express MHC class I molecules but avoid rejection by the maternal immune system.

441
00:53:29,907 --> 00:53:39,745
Moffett-King (2002) reasoned that normal implantation depends on controlled trophoblastic invasion of maternal decidua and spiral arteries.

442
00:53:39,745 --> 00:53:49,645
Such invasion must proceed far enough to provide for normal fetal growth and development but avoid the pathogenic invasion seen in placenta accreta spectrum disorders .

443
00:53:49,645 --> 00:54:02,070
She suggests that dNK cells combined with extravillous trophoblasts’ unique expression of three specific HLA class I genes act in concert to permit and subsequently limit trophoblast invasion.

444
00:54:02,070 --> 00:54:11,620
Extravillous trophoblasts express the class Ia antigen HLA-C and nonclassic class Ib molecules HLA-E and HLA-G.

445
00:54:11,620 --> 00:54:20,020
HLA-G antigen is expressed only in humans and is restricted to extravillous trophoblasts in contact with maternal tissues.

446
00:54:20,020 --> 00:54:39,707
Expressed in both membrane-bound and soluble isoforms detectable in maternal circulation, HLA-G is thought to protect extravillous trophoblasts from immune rejection by modulating the function of both decidual and circulating NK populations (Apps, 2011; Rajagopalan, 2012).

447
00:54:39,707 --> 00:54:49,332
The importance of this molecule is highlighted by the observation that IVF embryos fail to implant if they do not express a soluble HLA-G isoform .

448
00:54:49,332 --> 00:54:56,595
Thus, HLA-G may act through multiple mechanisms to aid tolerance of the maternal–fetal antigen mismatch .

449
00:54:56,595 --> 00:55:05,657
Abnormal HLA-G expression in extravillous trophoblasts from women with preeclampsia suggests immune dysregulation as one etiology .

450
00:55:05,657 --> 00:55:15,895
Decidual Immune Cells Of leukocytes, NK cells predominate in midluteal phase endometrium and in first-trimester decidua, but numbers decline by term .

451
00:55:15,895 --> 00:55:23,995
In firsttrimester decidua, dNK cells lie close to extravillous trophoblasts and purportedly regulate invasion.

452
00:55:23,995 --> 00:55:34,857
Their infiltration is increased by progesterone and by stromal cell production of IL-15 and decidual prolactin (Dunn, 2002; Gubbay, 2002).

453
00:55:34,857 --> 00:55:42,495
Although dNK cells have the capacity for cytotoxicity, they are not cytotoxic toward fetal trophoblasts.

454
00:55:42,495 --> 00:55:48,382
Their cytotoxic potential is prevented by molecular cues from decidual macrophages.

455
00:55:48,382 --> 00:55:55,607
As noted, specific HLA molecule expression protects against dNK cells’ damaging actions.

456
00:55:55,607 --> 00:56:05,270
Decidual macrophages are another decidual immune cell type and are distinct from proinflammatory M1 or antiinflammatory M2 macrophages.

457
00:56:05,270 --> 00:56:17,695
These cells regulate adaptive T cell responses; control dNK differentiation, activation, and cytotoxicity; and produce antiinflammatory cytokines such as IL-10.

458
00:56:17,695 --> 00:56:22,820
Dendritic cells are antigen-presenting cells that educate maternal T cells.

459
00:56:22,820 --> 00:56:27,495
They affect development of a receptive endometrium for implantation.

460
00:56:27,495 --> 00:56:36,007
Maternal T cells, as part of the adaptive immune response, increase in number and function after encounter with a specific antigen.

461
00:56:36,007 --> 00:56:41,945
These cells subsequently retain the ability to respond rapidly in a subsequent encounter

462
00:56:42,100 --> 00:56:44,237
with the same antigen.

463
00:56:44,187 --> 00:56:51,325
In contrast, Tregs are immunosuppressive, and during pregnancy systemic maternal populations expand.

464
00:56:51,325 --> 00:56:57,350
Specific Treg cell populations persist and protect against aberrant immune responses.

465
00:56:57,350 --> 00:56:58,962
Amnion.

466
00:56:58,962 --> 00:57:03,700
At term, the amnion is a tough and tenacious but pliable membrane.

467
00:57:03,700 --> 00:57:12,762
This innermost avascular fetal membrane is contiguous with amnionic fluid and provides almost all of the tensile strength of the fetal membranes.

468
00:57:12,762 --> 00:57:17,612
Its resilience to rupture is vital to successful pregnancy outcomes.

469
00:57:17,612 --> 00:57:23,375
Indeed, preterm rupture of fetal membranes is a major cause of preterm delivery .

470
00:57:23,375 --> 00:57:28,412
Bourne (1962) described five separate amnion layers.

471
00:57:28,412 --> 00:57:33,912
Here, progression of discussed layers moves from amnionic fluid to the chorion.

472
00:57:33,912 --> 00:57:40,475
The innermost layer, which is bathed by amnionic fluid, is a single-layer cuboidal epithelium .

473
00:57:40,475 --> 00:57:45,075
This epithelium attaches firmly to a distinct basement membrane.

474
00:57:45,075 --> 00:57:53,937
Next, an acellular compact layer composed primarily of interstitial collagens is followed by the fibroblast-like mesenchymal cell layer.

475
00:57:53,937 --> 00:58:01,062
The outermost layer is the relatively acellular zona spongiosa, which is contiguous with the chorion laeve.

476
00:58:01,062 --> 00:58:07,600
The amnion also contains a few fetal macrophages, which predominate in the outer two layers.

477
00:58:07,600 --> 00:58:14,125
The amnion lacks smooth muscle cells, nerves, lymphatics, and importantly, blood vessels.

478
00:58:14,125 --> 00:58:16,700
Figure 5-14.

479
00:58:16,700 --> 00:58:19,775
Photomicrograph of fetal membranes.

480
00:58:19,775 --> 00:58:34,062
From left to right: AE = amnion epithelium; AM = amnion mesenchyme; S = zona spongiosa; CM = chorionic mesenchyme; TR = trophoblast; D = decidua.

481
00:58:34,062 --> 00:58:39,912
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Judith R. Head.) # Amnion Development.

482
00:58:39,912 --> 00:58:49,137
Early during implantation, a space develops between the embryonic cell mass and adjacent trophoblastic cells (see Fig. 5-7).

483
00:58:49,137 --> 00:59:00,662
Small cells that line this inner surface of trophoblasts are precursors of amnionic epithelium, and the amnion is first identifiable on the 7th or 8th day of embryo development.

484
00:59:00,662 --> 00:59:07,687
It is initially a minute vesicle, which then develops into a small sac that covers the dorsal embryo surface.

485
00:59:07,687 --> 00:59:14,525
As the amnion enlarges, it gradually engulfs the growing embryo, which prolapses into its cavity .

486
00:59:14,525 --> 00:59:21,575
Distention of the amnionic sac eventually brings it into contact with the interior surface of the chorion laeve.

487
00:59:21,575 --> 00:59:28,537
Apposition of the chorion laeve and amnion near the end of the first trimester obliterates the extraembryonic coelom.

488
00:59:28,537 --> 00:59:36,000
The amnion and chorion laeve, although slightly adhered, are never intimately connected and can be separated easily.

489
00:59:36,000 --> 00:59:42,537
Placental amnion covers the placental surface and thereby is in contact with the chorionic vessels.

490
00:59:42,537 --> 00:59:46,125
Umbilical amnion covers the umbilical cord.

491
00:59:46,125 --> 00:59:56,025
As discussed in Chapter 48 (p. 842), with monochorionic-diamnionic placentas, no tissue intervenes between the fused amnions.

492
00:59:56,025 --> 01:00:02,675
With dichorionic-diamnionic twin placentas, amnions are separated by fused chorion laeves.

493
01:00:02,675 --> 01:00:06,200
Amnionic fluid fills the amnionic sac.

494
01:00:06,200 --> 01:00:13,987
As pregnancy progresses, this normally clear fluid increases in volume until approximately 34 weeks’ gestation.

495
01:00:13,987 --> 01:00:17,025
After this, the volume declines.

496
01:00:17,025 --> 01:00:25,112
At term, it averages 1000 mL, although this may vary widely in normal and especially abnormal conditions.

497
01:00:25,112 --> 01:00:34,250
Amnionic fluid origin, composition, circulation, and function of are discussed further in Chapter 14 (p. 256).

498
01:00:34,250 --> 01:00:37,550
# Amnion Cell Histogenesis.

499
01:00:37,550 --> 01:00:44,087
Amnionic epithelium derives from fetal ectoderm of the embryonic disc and not from trophoblasts.

500
01:00:44,087 --> 01:00:49,000
This is an important consideration both embryologically and functionally.

501
01:00:49,000 --> 01:00:57,462
For example, HLA class I gene expression in amnion is more akin to that in embryonic cells than to that in trophoblasts.

502
01:00:57,462 --> 01:01:03,087
The fibroblast-like mesenchymal cell layer likely originates from embryonic mesoderm.

503
01:01:03,087 --> 01:01:11,875
Early in human embryogenesis, the amnionic mesenchymal cells lie immediately adjacent to the basal surface of the amnion epithelium.

504
01:01:11,875 --> 01:01:19,512
At this time, the amnion has two-cell layers and approximately equal numbers of epithelial and mesenchymal cells.

505
01:01:19,512 --> 01:01:26,700
Simultaneously with growth and development, interstitial collagens are deposited between these two cell layers.

506
01:01:26,700 --> 01:01:32,812
This marks formation of the amnion compact layer, which separates the two early layers.

507
01:01:32,812 --> 01:01:36,225
Amnionic epithelium early in pregnancy

508
01:01:36,388 --> 01:01:40,775
replicates at a rate appreciably faster than mesenchymal cells.

509
01:01:40,725 --> 01:01:47,925
Thus, as the amnionic sac expands, its epithelial cells form a continuous, uninterrupted layer.

510
01:01:47,925 --> 01:01:52,500
Instead, mesenchymal cells become more sparsely distributed.

511
01:01:52,500 --> 01:01:59,100
Connected by a lattice network of extracellular matrix, they appear as long, slender fibrils.

512
01:01:59,100 --> 01:02:06,525
Amnion Epithelial Cells The apical surface of the amnionic epithelium is replete with highly developed microvilli.

513
01:02:06,525 --> 01:02:13,700
This structure reflects its function as a major site of transfer between amnionic fluid and amnion layers.

514
01:02:13,700 --> 01:02:25,725
This epithelium is metabolically active, and its cells synthesize tissue inhibitor of MMP-1, prostaglandin E2 (PGE2), and fetal fibronectin (fFN) .

515
01:02:25,725 --> 01:02:33,163
Although epithelia produce fFN, studies suggest that fFN acts in the underlying mesenchymal cells.

516
01:02:33,163 --> 01:02:39,238
Here, fFN promotes synthesis of MMPs that break down strength-bearing collagens.

517
01:02:39,238 --> 01:02:44,563
It also enhances prostaglandin synthesis to prompt uterine contractions .

518
01:02:44,563 --> 01:02:57,038
This pathway is upregulated with premature rupture of membranes induced by thrombin or infectioninduced release of fFN (Chigusa, 2016; Mogami, 2014).

519
01:02:57,038 --> 01:03:06,325
Epithelial cells may respond to signals derived from the fetus or the mother, and they are responsive to various endocrine or paracrine modulators.

520
01:03:06,325 --> 01:03:13,450
Examples include oxytocin and vasopressin, both of which increase PGE2 production in vitro .

521
01:03:13,450 --> 01:03:19,038
These cells may also produce cytokines such as IL-8 during labor initiation .

522
01:03:19,038 --> 01:03:29,013
Amnionic epithelium also synthesizes vasoactive peptides, which function in both maternal and fetal tissues in diverse physiological processes.

523
01:03:29,013 --> 01:03:38,388
These peptides include endothelin and parathyroid hormone-related protein (Economos, 1992; Germain, 1992).

524
01:03:38,388 --> 01:03:51,738
Others are brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) and corticotropin-releasing hormone, which are peptides that invoke smooth-muscle relaxation (Riley, 1991; Warren, 1995).

525
01:03:51,738 --> 01:04:00,225
BNP production is positively regulated by mechanical stretch in fetal membranes and is proposed to function in uterine quiescence.

526
01:04:00,225 --> 01:04:10,750
Epidermal growth factor, a negative regulator of BNP, is upregulated in the membranes at term and leads to a decline in BNP-regulated uterine quiescence .

527
01:04:10,750 --> 01:04:16,050
Amnion Mesenchymal Cells These cells are responsible for other major functions.

528
01:04:16,050 --> 01:04:24,713
Mesenchymal cells synthesize the interstitial collagens that compose the amnionic compact layer —the major source of its tensile strength .

529
01:04:24,713 --> 01:04:34,438
At term, the generation of cortisol by 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase may contribute to membrane rupture by reducing collagen abundance .

530
01:04:34,438 --> 01:04:41,813
Mesenchymal cells also synthesize cytokines that include IL-6, IL-8, and MCP-1.

531
01:04:41,813 --> 01:04:47,538
Cytokine synthesis rises in response to bacterial toxins and IL-1.

532
01:04:47,538 --> 01:05:00,025
This ability of amnion mesenchymal cells to synthesize chemokines is an important consideration in interpreting studies of labor-associated accumulation of inflammatory mediators in amnionic fluid .

533
01:05:00,025 --> 01:05:12,900
Last, mesenchymal cells may be a greater source of PGE2 than epithelial cells, especially in the case of premature membrane rupture (Mogami, 2013; Whittle, 2000).

534
01:05:12,900 --> 01:05:15,300
# Tensile Strength.

535
01:05:15,300 --> 01:05:22,613
During tests of tensile strength, the decidua and then the chorion laeve give way long before the amnion ruptures.

536
01:05:22,613 --> 01:05:28,650
Indeed, the membranes are elastic and can expand to twice normal size during pregnancy .

537
01:05:28,650 --> 01:05:40,538
The amnion tensile strength resides almost exclusively in the compact layer, which is composed of cross-linked interstitial collagens I and III, and lesser amounts of collagens V and VI.

538
01:05:40,538 --> 01:05:45,863
Amnion tensile strength is regulated in part by fibrillar collagen assembly.

539
01:05:45,863 --> 01:05:52,925
This process is influenced by the interaction between fibrils and proteoglycans such as decorin and biglycan .

540
01:05:52,925 --> 01:06:01,850
Reduction of these proteoglycans is reported to perturb fetal membrane function (Horgan, 2014; Wu, 2014).

541
01:06:01,850 --> 01:06:10,913
Fetal membranes overlying the cervix have a regional shift in gene expression and lymphocyte activation that set in motion an inflammatory cascade .

542
01:06:10,913 --> 01:06:16,638
This change may contribute to tissue remodeling and loss of tensile strength in the amnion .

543
01:06:16,638 --> 01:06:19,425
# Metabolic Functions.

544
01:06:19,425 --> 01:06:29,225
The amnion is metabolically active, is involved in solute and water transport for amnionic fluid homeostasis, and produces an impressive array

545
01:06:29,380 --> 01:06:32,030
of bioactive compounds.

546
01:06:31,980 --> 01:06:43,030
The amnion is responsive both acutely and chronically to mechanical stretch, which alters amnionic gene expression (Carvajal, 2013; Nemeth, 2000).

547
01:06:43,030 --> 01:06:56,067
This in turn may trigger both autocrine and paracrine responses that include production of MMPs, IL-8, and collagenase (BryantGreenwood, 1998; Mogami, 2013).

548
01:06:56,067 --> 01:07:00,905
Such factors may modulate changes in membrane properties during labor.

549
01:07:00,905 --> 01:07:02,867
Umbilical Cord.

550
01:07:02,867 --> 01:07:08,767
The yolk sac and the umbilical vesicle into which it develops are prominent early in pregnancy.

551
01:07:08,767 --> 01:07:16,617
Initially, the embryo is a flattened disc interposed between amnion and yolk sac (see Fig. 5-7).

552
01:07:16,617 --> 01:07:24,792
The embryonic dorsal surface, in association with the elongation of its neural tube, grows faster than the ventral surface.

553
01:07:24,792 --> 01:07:33,392
As a result, the embryo bulges into the amnionic sac, and the embryo body incorporates the adjacent yolk sac to form the gut.

554
01:07:33,392 --> 01:07:37,317
The body stalk connects the caudal embryo to the chorion.

555
01:07:37,317 --> 01:07:44,955
The fetal allantois forms as a diverticulum from the caudal wall of the yolk sac and projects into the base of the body stalk.

556
01:07:44,955 --> 01:07:51,167
As pregnancy advances, the yolk sac becomes smaller and its pedicle relatively longer.

557
01:07:51,167 --> 01:07:58,680
By the middle of the third month, the expanding amnion fuses with the chorion laeve to obliterate the extraembryonic coelom.

558
01:07:58,680 --> 01:08:05,580
In its expansion, the amnion covers the bulging placental disc and the lateral surface of the body stalk.

559
01:08:05,580 --> 01:08:09,480
The latter is then called the umbilical cord or funis.

560
01:08:09,480 --> 01:08:16,942
A more detailed description of this cord and potential abnormalities is found in Chapter 6 (p. 113).

561
01:08:16,942 --> 01:08:21,180
The cord at term normally has two arteries and one vein.

562
01:08:21,180 --> 01:08:28,555
The right umbilical vein usually disappears early during fetal development, leaving only the original left vein.

563
01:08:28,555 --> 01:08:36,230
The umbilical cord extends from the fetal umbilicus to the fetal surface of the placenta, that is, the chorionic plate.

564
01:08:36,230 --> 01:08:44,167
As discussed in detail in Chapter 7 (p. 126), blood flows from the umbilical vein toward the fetus.

565
01:08:44,167 --> 01:08:49,192
Blood then takes a path of least resistance via two routes within the fetus.

566
01:08:49,192 --> 01:08:54,830
One is the ductus venosus, which empties directly into the inferior vena cava.

567
01:08:54,830 --> 01:09:00,317
The other route consists of numerous smaller openings into the hepatic circulation.

568
01:09:00,317 --> 01:09:05,530
Blood from the liver flows into the hepatic vein and then the inferior vena cava.

569
01:09:05,530 --> 01:09:15,280
Resistance in the ductus venosus is controlled by a sphincter situated at the origin of the ductus at the umbilical recess and is innervated by a vagus nerve branch.

570
01:09:15,280 --> 01:09:19,255
Blood exits the fetus via the two umbilical arteries.

571
01:09:19,255 --> 01:09:27,542
These are anterior branches of the internal iliac artery and become obliterated after birth to form the medial umbilical ligaments.

572
01:09:27,542 --> 01:09:29,730
Placental Hormones.

573
01:09:29,730 --> 01:09:40,242
The production of steroid and protein hormones by human trophoblasts is greater in amount and diversity than that of any single endocrine tissue in all of mammalian physiology.

574
01:09:40,242 --> 01:09:48,830
Table 5-1 is a compendium of average production rates for various steroid hormones in nonpregnant and in near-term pregnant women.

575
01:09:48,830 --> 01:09:54,530
It demonstrates the remarkable increase in steroid hormone production during pregnancy.

576
01:09:54,530 --> 01:10:02,667
The human placenta also synthesizes an enormous amount of protein and peptide hormones, summarized in Table 5-2.

577
01:10:02,667 --> 01:10:10,580
The successful physiological adaptations of pregnant women to this unique endocrine milieu is discussed throughout Chapter 4.

578
01:10:10,580 --> 01:10:12,805
Table 5-1.

579
01:10:12,805 --> 01:10:18,755
Steroid Production Rates in Nonpregnant and Near-Term Pregnant Women Table 5-2.

580
01:10:18,755 --> 01:10:24,767
Protein Hormones Produced by the Human Placenta # Human Chorionic Gonadotropin.

581
01:10:24,767 --> 01:10:35,192
Biosynthesis Chorionic gonadotropin is a glycoprotein with biological activity similar to that of LH, and both act via the LH-hCG receptor.

582
01:10:35,192 --> 01:10:46,505
hCG varies in molecular weight from 36,000 to 40,000 Da and is highly glycosylated with the most carbohydrate content of any human hormone—30 percent.

583
01:10:46,505 --> 01:10:57,067
This glycosylation protects the molecule from catabolism and results in a 36-hour plasma halflife for intact hCG compared with 2 hours for LH.

584
01:10:57,067 --> 01:11:02,855
The hCG molecule is composed of two dissimilar subunits, α and β.

585
01:11:02,855 --> 01:11:08,867
These are noncovalently linked but held together by electrostatic and hydrophobic forces.

586
01:11:08,867 --> 01:11:16,055
Isolated subunits are unable to bind the LH-hCG receptor and thus lack biological activity.

587
01:11:16,204 --> 01:11:26,341
The hCG hormone is structurally related to three other glycoprotein hormones—LH, FSH, and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH).

588
01:11:26,291 --> 01:11:30,266
All four glycoproteins share a common α-subunit.

589
01:11:30,266 --> 01:11:37,566
However, each of their β-subunits is characterized by a distinct (although related) amino acid sequence.

590
01:11:37,566 --> 01:11:45,179
Synthesis of the α- and β-chains of hCG is regulated separately from gene loci on different chromosomes.

591
01:11:45,179 --> 01:11:49,616
A single gene on chromosome 6 encodes the α-subunit.

592
01:11:49,616 --> 01:11:58,804
Chromosome 19 encodes the β-hCG–β-LH family of subunits with six genes for β-hCG and one for β-LH .

593
01:11:58,804 --> 01:12:05,854
Both subunits are synthesized as larger precursors and then cleaved by endopeptidases to their mature form.

594
01:12:05,854 --> 01:12:11,616
Intact hCG is assembled and released by secretory granule exocytosis .

595
01:12:11,616 --> 01:12:24,266
Modifications during synthesis and subsequent enzymatic degradation give rise to multiple forms of hCG in maternal plasma and urine that vary enormously in bioactivity and immunoreactivity.

596
01:12:24,266 --> 01:12:31,091
Before 5 weeks, hCG is expressed both in the syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblasts .

597
01:12:31,091 --> 01:12:46,729
Later in the first trimester, hCG is produced almost solely in the syncytiotrophoblast, peaks around 9 weeks’ gestation, and then declines to a plateau for the remainder of gestation (Beck, 1986; Kurman, 1984).

598
01:12:46,729 --> 01:12:58,991
Dynamic changes in hCG concentration in the first trimester highlight the importance of accurate gestational age estimation when interpreting aneuploidy screening strategies that include hCG.

599
01:12:58,991 --> 01:13:09,941
Circulating levels of free β-subunit are low to undetectable throughout pregnancy, and their concentration is the limiting factor for secretion of complete hCG molecules.

600
01:13:09,941 --> 01:13:19,566
Levels of the α-subunit rise gradually and roughly correspond to placental mass, until they plateau at approximately 36 weeks’ gestation .

601
01:13:19,566 --> 01:13:30,979
Concentrations in Serum and Urine The combined hCG molecule is detectable in plasma of pregnant women 7 to 9 days after the midcycle LH surge preceding ovulation.

602
01:13:30,979 --> 01:13:37,041
Thus, hCG likely enters maternal blood at the time of blastocyst implantation.

603
01:13:37,041 --> 01:13:43,329
Plasma levels rise rapidly, doubling approximately every 2 days in the first trimester .

604
01:13:43,329 --> 01:13:48,916
Appreciable fluctuations in levels for a given patient are observed on the same day.

605
01:13:48,916 --> 01:13:51,466
Figure 5-15.

606
01:13:51,466 --> 01:14:06,191
Distinct profiles for the concentrations of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), human placental lactogen (hPL), and corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) in serum of women throughout normal pregnancy.

607
01:14:06,191 --> 01:14:14,879
Intact hCG circulates as multiple, highly related isoforms that have variable cross-reactivity between commercial assays.

608
01:14:14,879 --> 01:14:27,616
This emphasizes the need to use the same assay type when measuring serial hCG levels for clinical indications such as evaluating pregnancy of unknown location or medical management of ectopic pregnancy.

609
01:14:27,616 --> 01:14:37,166
Peak maternal plasma levels reach approximately 50,000 to 100,000 mIU/mL between the 60th and 80th days after menses.

610
01:14:37,166 --> 01:14:43,704
Plasma levels then decline, and a nadir is reached by approximately 16 weeks’ gestation.

611
01:14:43,704 --> 01:14:48,166
Plasma levels remain at this lower level for the rest of pregnancy.

612
01:14:48,166 --> 01:15:00,966
Although maternal urine, like plasma, contains various hCG degradation products, the principal urinary form is the terminal product of hCG degradation, namely, the β-core fragment.

613
01:15:00,966 --> 01:15:09,554
Concentrations of this fragment follow the same general pattern as that in maternal plasma, peaking at approximately 10 weeks’ gestation.

614
01:15:09,554 --> 01:15:22,841
Importantly, the β-subunit antibody used in most pregnancy tests reacts with both intact hCG (the major form in the plasma) and with fragments of hCG (the major form found in urine).

615
01:15:22,841 --> 01:15:29,841
hCG Regulation Placental GnRH is likely involved in the regulation of hCG formation.

616
01:15:29,841 --> 01:15:36,441
Both GnRH and its receptor are expressed by cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblast .

617
01:15:36,441 --> 01:15:51,579
GnRH administration elevates circulating hCG levels, and cultured trophoblasts respond to GnRH treatment with increased hCG secretion (Iwashita, 1993; Siler-Khodr, 1981).

618
01:15:51,579 --> 01:15:56,741
Pituitary GnRH production is regulated by inhibin and activin.

619
01:15:56,741 --> 01:16:09,104
Likewise, in cultured placental cells, activin stimulates and inhibin inhibits GnRH and hCG production (Petraglia, 1989; Steele, 1993).

620
01:16:09,104 --> 01:16:15,354
hCG is cleared by the kidney (about 30 percent), and the remainder is likely cleared by

621
01:16:15,508 --> 01:16:17,608
liver metabolism .

622
01:16:17,558 --> 01:16:22,808
Thus, levels can be markedly altered in gravidas with chronic renal disease.

623
01:16:22,808 --> 01:16:31,383
Biological Functions Noted earlier, hCG maintains corpus luteum function—that is, continued progesterone production.

624
01:16:31,383 --> 01:16:38,208
Both hCG subunits are required for binding to the LH-hCG receptor in the corpus luteum.

625
01:16:38,208 --> 01:16:48,095
However, maximum plasma hCG concentrations are attained well after hCG-stimulated corpus luteum secretion of progesterone has ceased.

626
01:16:48,095 --> 01:16:58,145
Specifically, luteal progesterone synthesis begins to decline at approximately 6 weeks’ gestation despite continued and increasing hCG production.

627
01:16:58,145 --> 01:17:04,808
Therefore, this incompletely explains the physiological function of hCG in pregnancy.

628
01:17:04,808 --> 01:17:11,720
LH-hCG receptors are present in various other tissues, and roles are discussed subsequently.

629
01:17:11,720 --> 01:17:18,733
In pregnancies with male fetuses, hCG stimulates fetal testicular testosterone secretion.

630
01:17:18,733 --> 01:17:22,633
This reaches a maximum when hCG levels peak.

631
01:17:22,633 --> 01:17:30,945
Thus, at a critical time in male sexual differentiation, hCG enters fetal plasma from the syncytiotrophoblast.

632
01:17:30,945 --> 01:17:40,570
In the fetus, it acts as an LH surrogate to stimulate Leydig cell replication and testosterone synthesis to promote male sexual differentiation .

633
01:17:40,570 --> 01:17:51,020
Before approximately 110 days, the fetal anterior pituitary lacks vascularization from the hypothalamus and produces minimal LH secretion.

634
01:17:51,020 --> 01:17:58,458
Thereafter, as hCG levels fall, pituitary LH maintains modest testicular stimulation.

635
01:17:58,458 --> 01:18:04,045
The maternal thyroid gland also is stimulated by large quantities of hCG.

636
01:18:04,045 --> 01:18:12,320
In women with gestational trophoblastic disease, biochemical and clinical evidence of hyperthyroidism sometimes develops .

637
01:18:12,320 --> 01:18:17,395
Some forms of hCG bind to TSH receptors on thyrocytes .

638
01:18:17,395 --> 01:18:25,083
The thyroid-stimulatory activity in plasma of first-trimester pregnant women varies appreciably from sample to sample.

639
01:18:25,083 --> 01:18:33,445
Modifications of hCG oligosaccharides likely are important in the capacity of hCG to stimulate thyroid function.

640
01:18:33,445 --> 01:18:45,045
Acidic isoforms stimulate thyroid activity, and some more basic isoforms stimulate iodine uptake (Kraiem, 1994; Tsuruta, 1995).

641
01:18:45,045 --> 01:18:56,670
Last, the LH-hCG receptor is also expressed by thyrocytes, which suggests that hCG stimulates thyroid activity via the LH-hCG receptor as well .

642
01:18:56,670 --> 01:19:02,333
LH-hCG receptors are found in myometrium and in uterine vascular tissue.

643
01:19:02,333 --> 01:19:10,683
It has been hypothesized that hCG may promote uterine vascular vasodilation and myometrial smooth muscle relaxation .

644
01:19:10,683 --> 01:19:21,970
hCG also modulates maternal immune cell functions in the decidua during early stages of placentation (Schumacher, 2019; Silasi, 2020).

645
01:19:21,970 --> 01:19:30,208
Abnormally High or Low Levels Several clinical circumstances display substantively higher maternal plasma hCG levels.

646
01:19:30,208 --> 01:19:40,470
Some examples include multifetal pregnancy, erythroblastosis fetalis associated with fetal hemolytic anemia, and gestational trophoblastic disease.

647
01:19:40,470 --> 01:19:53,145
Relatively higher hCG levels may be found in women carrying a fetus with Down syndrome, and this has been incorporated into screening strategies (Table 17-4, p. 336).

648
01:19:53,145 --> 01:20:01,345
Various malignant tumors also produce hCG, sometimes in large amounts—especially trophoblastic neoplasms .

649
01:20:01,345 --> 01:20:08,795
Relatively lower hCG plasma levels are found in women with failing early pregnancies and ectopic pregnancy .

650
01:20:08,795 --> 01:20:18,220
hCG is produced in very small amounts in normal tissues of men and nonpregnant women, perhaps primarily in the anterior pituitary gland.

651
01:20:18,220 --> 01:20:24,833
Nonetheless, the detection of hCG in blood or urine almost always indicates pregnancy .

652
01:20:24,833 --> 01:20:27,883
# Human Placental Lactogen.

653
01:20:27,883 --> 01:20:42,870
Biosynthesis This single, nonglycosylated polypeptide chain shares a 96-percent aminoacid-sequence homology with human growth hormone (hGH) and a 67percent homology with human prolactin (hPRL).

654
01:20:42,870 --> 01:20:49,295
Because of these similarities, it was called chorionic growth hormone or human placental lactogen.

655
01:20:49,295 --> 01:20:52,783
Currently, the latter term is used by most.

656
01:20:52,783 --> 01:21:05,908
Five genes in the growth hormone–placental lactogen gene cluster are linked and located on chromosome 17: GH1, GH2, CSHL1, CSH1, and CSH2.

657
01:21:05,908 --> 01:21:11,383
Human placental lactogen (hPL) is encoded by the last two genes.

658
01:21:11,383 --> 01:21:25,133
hPL is concentrated in syncytiotrophoblast, but similar to hCG, hPL is demonstrated in cytotrophoblasts before 6 weeks (Grumbach, 1964; Maruo, 1992).

659
01:21:25,133 --> 01:21:27,920
Within 5 to 10 days after

660
01:21:28,084 --> 01:21:35,521
conception, hPL is demonstrable in the placenta and can be detected in maternal serum as early as 3 weeks.

661
01:21:35,471 --> 01:21:42,646
Levels of mRNA for hPL in syncytiotrophoblast remain relatively constant throughout pregnancy.

662
01:21:42,646 --> 01:21:49,034
This finding supports the idea that the hPL secretion rate is proportional to placental mass.

663
01:21:49,034 --> 01:21:53,896
Levels rise steadily until 34 to 36 weeks’ gestation.

664
01:21:53,896 --> 01:22:02,271
The hPL production rate near term—approximately 1 g/d— is by far the greatest of any known hormone in humans.

665
01:22:02,271 --> 01:22:07,046
It is rapidly cleared and has a half-life between 10 and 30 minutes .

666
01:22:07,046 --> 01:22:16,171
In late pregnancy, maternal serum concentrations reach levels of 5 to 15 μg/mL (see Fig. 5-15).

667
01:22:16,171 --> 01:22:24,321
Very little hPL is detected in fetal blood, and amnionic fluid levels are somewhat lower than that in maternal plasma.

668
01:22:24,321 --> 01:22:38,396
Thus, although hPL may have a direct effect on fetal tissues, such as modulating fetal vasculature formation, its primary role is to act on maternal physiology to ensure adequate nutrient delivery to the placenta .

669
01:22:38,396 --> 01:22:45,259
Metabolic Actions hPL has putative actions in several important maternal metabolic processes.

670
01:22:45,259 --> 01:22:51,246
First, hPL promotes lipolysis to raise circulating free fatty acid levels.

671
01:22:51,246 --> 01:22:56,296
This provides an energy source for maternal metabolism and fetal nutrition.

672
01:22:56,296 --> 01:23:03,034
In vitro studies suggest that hPL inhibits secretion by term syncytiotrophoblast of leptin .

673
01:23:03,034 --> 01:23:09,959
Prolonged maternal starvation in the first half of pregnancy leads to higher hPL plasma concentrations.

674
01:23:09,959 --> 01:23:15,634
Second, hPL aids maternal adaptation to fetal energy requirements .

675
01:23:15,634 --> 01:23:21,821
For example, increased maternal insulin resistance ensures nutrient flow to the fetus.

676
01:23:21,821 --> 01:23:28,434
It also favors protein synthesis and provides a readily available amino acid source to the fetus.

677
01:23:28,434 --> 01:23:36,096
To counterbalance the greater insulin resistance and prevent maternal hyperglycemia, maternal insulin levels rise.

678
01:23:36,096 --> 01:23:45,459
Both hPL and prolactin signal through the prolactin receptor to increase maternal beta cell proliferation, which augments insulin secretion .

679
01:23:45,459 --> 01:23:53,309
In animals, prolactin and hPL upregulate serotonin synthesis, which increases beta cell proliferation .

680
01:23:53,309 --> 01:24:01,471
Short-term changes in plasma glucose or insulin, however, have relatively little effect on plasma hPL levels.

681
01:24:01,471 --> 01:24:18,259
In vitro studies of syncytiotrophoblast suggest that hPL synthesis is stimulated by insulin and insulin-like growth factor-1 and inhibited by PGE2 and PGF2α (Bhaumick, 1987; Genbacev, 1977).

682
01:24:18,259 --> 01:24:21,634
# Other Placental Protein Hormones.

683
01:24:21,634 --> 01:24:32,509
The placenta has a remarkable capacity to synthesize numerous peptide hormones, including some that are analogous or related to hypothalamic and pituitary hormones.

684
01:24:32,509 --> 01:24:40,696
In contrast to their counterparts, some of these placental peptide/protein hormones are not subject to feedback inhibition.

685
01:24:40,696 --> 01:24:58,496
Hypothalamic-Like Releasing Hormones The known hypothalamic-releasing or -inhibiting hormones include GnRH, corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), growth hormone–releasing hormone (GHRH), and somatostatin.

686
01:24:58,496 --> 01:25:07,571
For each of these, the human placenta produces an analogous hormone (Petraglia, 1992; Siler-Khodr, 1988).

687
01:25:07,571 --> 01:25:16,209
GnRH in the placenta shows its highest expression in the first trimester (Siler-Khodr, 1978, 1988).

688
01:25:16,209 --> 01:25:21,846
Interestingly, it is found in cytotrophoblasts but not syncytiotrophoblast.

689
01:25:21,846 --> 01:25:33,146
Placenta-derived GnRH functions to regulate trophoblast hCG production and extravillous trophoblast invasion via regulation of MMP-2 and MMP-9 .

690
01:25:33,146 --> 01:25:40,159
Placenta-derived GnRH is also the likely source of elevated maternal GnRH levels in pregnancy .

691
01:25:40,159 --> 01:25:47,509
CRH is a member of a larger family of CRH-related peptides that includes CRH and urocortins .

692
01:25:47,509 --> 01:26:04,871
Maternal serum CRH levels rise from 5 to 10 pmol/L in the nonpregnant woman to approximately 100 pmol/L in the early third trimester and then to almost 500 pmol/L abruptly during the last 5 to 6 weeks (see Fig. 5-15).

693
01:26:04,871 --> 01:26:13,696
After labor begins, maternal plasma CRH levels rise even further (Petraglia, 1989, 1990).

694
01:26:13,696 --> 01:26:24,659
Urocortin, involved in the stress response, is also produced by the placenta and secreted into the maternal circulation, but at much lower levels than that seen for CRH .

695
01:26:24,659 --> 01:26:31,634
The function of CRH synthesized in the placenta, membranes, and decidua has been somewhat defined.

696
01:26:31,634 --> 01:26:33,146
For

697
01:26:33,316 --> 01:26:43,503
example, trophoblast, amniochorion, and decidua express both CRH-R1 and CRH-R2 receptors and several variant receptors .

698
01:26:43,453 --> 01:26:53,641
Both CRH and urocortin enhance trophoblast secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), and this suggests an autocrine–paracrine role .

699
01:26:53,641 --> 01:26:57,941
Large amounts of trophoblast CRH enter maternal blood.

700
01:26:57,941 --> 01:27:03,216
CRH receptors are also present in many tissues outside the placenta.

701
01:27:03,216 --> 01:27:11,916
Proposed biological roles include induction of smooth-muscle relaxation in vascular and myometrial tissue and immunosuppression.

702
01:27:11,916 --> 01:27:21,541
The physiological reverse, however, induction of myometrial contractions, has been proposed for the rising CRH levels seen near term.

703
01:27:21,541 --> 01:27:27,128
Some hypothesize that CRH may be involved with parturition initiation .

704
01:27:27,128 --> 01:27:32,216
Glucocorticoids act in the hypothalamus to inhibit CRH release.

705
01:27:32,216 --> 01:27:42,141
But, in the trophoblast, glucocorticoids stimulate CRH gene expression (Jones, 1989a; Robinson, 1988).

706
01:27:42,141 --> 01:28:01,553
Thus, a novel positive feedback loop in the placenta may allow placental CRH to stimulate placental ACTH and thereby prompt fetal and maternal adrenal glucocorticoid production, with subsequent stimulation of placental CRH expression (Nicholson, 2001; Riley, 1991).

707
01:28:01,553 --> 01:28:06,578
GHRH is expressed in placenta, but its function is unclear .

708
01:28:06,578 --> 01:28:13,028
GHRH may play an autocrine role in trophoblast survival via the GHRH receptor .

709
01:28:13,028 --> 01:28:18,753
Ghrelin is another regulator of hGH secretion and is produced by placental tissue .

710
01:28:18,753 --> 01:28:34,678
Trophoblast ghrelin expression peaks at midpregnancy and is a paracrine regulator of differentiation or is a potential regulator of human growth hormone variant production, described next (Fuglsang, 2005; Gualillo, 2001).

711
01:28:34,678 --> 01:28:43,666
Pituitary-Like Hormones A human growth hormone variant (hGH-V) that is not expressed in the pituitary is expressed in the placenta.

712
01:28:43,666 --> 01:28:50,991
The gene encoding hGH-V is located in the hGH–hPL gene cluster on chromosome 17.

713
01:28:50,991 --> 01:29:02,778
Sometimes referred to as placental growth hormone, hGH-V is a 191-amino-acid protein that differs in 15 amino acid positions from the sequence for hGH.

714
01:29:02,778 --> 01:29:14,903
Although hGH-V retains growth-promoting and antilipogenic functions that are similar to those of hGH, it has reduced diabetogenic and lactogenic functions relative to hGH .

715
01:29:14,903 --> 01:29:20,428
Placental hGH-V presumably is synthesized in the syncytiotrophoblast.

716
01:29:20,428 --> 01:29:33,803
It is believed that hGH-V is present in maternal plasma by 21 to 26 weeks’ gestation, rises in concentration until approximately 36 weeks, and remains relatively constant thereafter.

717
01:29:33,803 --> 01:29:40,616
hGH-V levels in maternal plasma and those of insulin-like growth factor 1 positively correlate.

718
01:29:40,616 --> 01:29:48,191
Moreover, hGH-V secretion by trophoblast in vitro is inhibited by glucose in a dose-dependent manner .

719
01:29:48,191 --> 01:29:57,928
Overexpression of hGH-V in mice causes severe insulin resistance, making it a likely candidate to mediate insulin resistance of pregnancy .

720
01:29:57,928 --> 01:30:06,166
Pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) is a polypeptide produced in the pituitary and other tissues including the placenta.

721
01:30:06,166 --> 01:30:19,966
It is proteolytically cleaved into numerous active hormones including ACTH, β-lipotropic hormone, melanocyte-stimulating hormone (α-, β-, and γ-MSH), and β–endorphin .

722
01:30:19,966 --> 01:30:24,053
These hormones play a role in maintaining energy balance.

723
01:30:24,053 --> 01:30:37,141
As discussed, placental CRH stimulates synthesis and release of placental ACTH, demonstrating the autocrine and paracrine functions of the placenta in addition to its systemic endocrine activity.

724
01:30:37,141 --> 01:30:43,191
Relaxin The peptide is expressed in human corpus luteum, decidua, and placenta .

725
01:30:43,191 --> 01:30:54,553
Two of the three relaxin genes—H2 and H3—are transcribed in the corpus luteum (Bathgate, 2002; Hudson, 1983, 1984).

726
01:30:54,553 --> 01:30:59,678
Decidua, placenta, and membranes express H1 and H2 .

727
01:30:59,678 --> 01:31:08,428
Relaxin is synthesized as a single, 105-amino-acid preprorelaxin molecule that is cleaved to A and B molecules.

728
01:31:08,428 --> 01:31:13,541
Relaxin is structurally similar to insulin and insulin-like growth factor.

729
01:31:13,541 --> 01:31:23,153
The rise in maternal circulating relaxin levels in early pregnancy is attributed to corpus luteum secretion, and levels parallel those of hCG.

730
01:31:23,153 --> 01:31:32,141
Relaxin, along with rising progesterone levels, may act on myometrium to promote relaxation and the quiescence of early pregnancy .

731
01:31:32,141 --> 01:31:38,366
In addition, the production of relaxin and relaxin-like factors within the placenta and fetal

732
01:31:38,524 --> 01:31:48,124
membranes may play an autocrine-paracrine role in postpartum regulation of extracellular matrix remodeling (Qin, 1997a,b).

733
01:31:48,074 --> 01:31:55,774
One important relaxin function is enhancement of the maternal glomerular filtration rate that is apparent early in gestation .

734
01:31:55,774 --> 01:32:07,361
Parathyroid Hormone-Related Protein Levels of this peptide are elevated within maternal but not fetal circulation (Bertelloni, 1994; Saxe, 1997).

735
01:32:07,361 --> 01:32:22,386
Parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP) synthesis is found in several normal adult tissues, especially in reproductive organs that include myometrium, endometrium, corpus luteum, and lactating mammary tissue.

736
01:32:22,386 --> 01:32:27,499
PTH-rP is not produced in the parathyroid glands of normal adults.

737
01:32:27,499 --> 01:32:36,211
Although yet undefined, placenta-derived PTH-rP may regulate genes involved in transfer of calcium and other solutes.

738
01:32:36,211 --> 01:32:43,299
It also contributes to mineral homeostasis in fetal bone, amnionic fluid, and the fetal circulation .

739
01:32:43,299 --> 01:32:52,149
Leptin This hormone is normally secreted by adipocytes, but cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblast also synthesize leptin .

740
01:32:52,149 --> 01:32:58,986
Relative contributions of leptin from maternal adipose tissue versus placenta are currently undefined.

741
01:32:58,986 --> 01:33:05,774
Leptin functions as an antiobesity hormone that decreases food intake through its hypothalamic receptor.

742
01:33:05,774 --> 01:33:12,974
It also regulates bone growth and immune function (Cock, 2003; La Cava, 2004).

743
01:33:12,974 --> 01:33:26,611
Placental leptin promotes placental cell proliferation, protein synthesis, and activation of immune tolerance and antiapoptotic responses (Rosario, 2016a; Schanton, 2018).

744
01:33:26,611 --> 01:33:31,674
Maternal serum levels are significantly higher than those in nonpregnant women.

745
01:33:31,674 --> 01:33:38,386
Fetal leptin levels correlate positively with birthweight and likely function in fetal development and growth.

746
01:33:38,386 --> 01:33:51,774
Studies suggest that reductions in leptin availability contribute to adverse fetal metabolic programing in intrauterine growth-restricted offspring (Nusken, 2016, PerezPerez, 2018).

747
01:33:51,774 --> 01:33:57,674
Neuropeptide Y This 36-amino-acid peptide is widely distributed in brain.

748
01:33:57,674 --> 01:34:06,811
It also is found in sympathetic neurons innervating the cardiovascular, respiratory, gastrointestinal, and genitourinary systems.

749
01:34:06,811 --> 01:34:12,899
Neuropeptide Y has been isolated from the placenta and localized in cytotrophoblasts .

750
01:34:12,899 --> 01:34:20,886
Trophoblasts possess neuropeptide Y receptors, and treatment of these with neuropeptide Y causes CRH release .

751
01:34:20,886 --> 01:34:33,636
Transforming Growth Factor Beta Superfamily This family of cytokines regulates various cellular functions that include placental development, trophoblast differentiation, and invasion into the decidua .

752
01:34:33,636 --> 01:34:45,274
This process is finely tuned, and extreme invasion results in placenta accreta spectrum disorders, whereas shallow implantation can lead to early pregnancy loss or preeclampsia.

753
01:34:45,274 --> 01:35:00,161
Members of the family include transforming growth factor beta (TGF-β), activin, inhibin, nodal, bone morphogenic proteins (BMPs), antimüllerian hormone, and growth differentiation factors (GDFs).

754
01:35:00,161 --> 01:35:09,499
These cytokines bind to a suite of receptors composed of a type 1 subunit and a type 2 receptor subunit to ultimately direct gene expression.

755
01:35:09,499 --> 01:35:15,486
This combinatorial diversity leads to divergent functions in the placenta and decidua.

756
01:35:15,486 --> 01:35:21,736
Activin A promotes syncytialization, extravillous trophoblast formation, and invasion.

757
01:35:21,736 --> 01:35:32,736
BMP2 enhances invasion, and BMP4 may direct embryonic stem cells toward a trophoblast lineage (Zhang 2018, Xu 2002).

758
01:35:32,736 --> 01:35:40,536
Inhibin A promotes syncytialization but inhibits invasion (Debiève, 2000; Jones, 2006).

759
01:35:40,536 --> 01:35:46,349
Nodal inhibits proliferation, extravillous trophoblast formation, and invasion.

760
01:35:46,349 --> 01:35:52,986
The complete role this superfamily in normal and abnormal placental development is yet to be fully defined.

761
01:35:52,986 --> 01:35:56,311
# Placental Progesterone Production.

762
01:35:56,311 --> 01:36:01,849
After 6 to 7 weeks’ gestation, little progesterone is produced in the ovary .

763
01:36:01,849 --> 01:36:14,886
Surgical removal of the corpus luteum or even bilateral oophorectomy during the 7th to 10th week does not decrease excretion rates of urinary pregnanediol, the principal urinary metabolite of progesterone.

764
01:36:14,886 --> 01:36:27,586
Before this time, however, corpus luteum removal will lead to spontaneous abortion unless an exogenous progestin is given, and Chapter 66 (p. 1170) lists suitable dosing.

765
01:36:27,586 --> 01:36:31,724
After approximately 8 weeks, the placenta assumes

766
01:36:31,876 --> 01:36:37,713
progesterone secretion, and maternal serum levels throughout pregnancy gradually rise .

767
01:36:37,663 --> 01:36:45,626
By term, these levels are 10 to 5000 times of those in nonpregnant women, depending on the ovarian cycle stage.

768
01:36:45,626 --> 01:36:48,176
Figure 5-16.

769
01:36:48,176 --> 01:36:56,188
Plasma levels of progesterone, estradiol, estrone, estetrol, and estriol in women during the course of gestation.

770
01:36:56,188 --> 01:37:05,288
Modified and redrawn with permission from Mesiano S: The endocrinology of human pregnancy and fetoplacental neuroendocrine development.

771
01:37:05,288 --> 01:37:20,113
In Strauss JF, Barbieri RL (eds) Yen and Jaffe’s Reproductive Endocrinology: Physiology, Pathophysiology, and Clinical Management, 6th ed. Philadephia, PA: Saunders; 2009.

772
01:37:20,113 --> 01:37:27,913
The daily production rate of progesterone in late, normal, singleton pregnancies approximates 250 mg.

773
01:37:27,913 --> 01:37:38,563
In multifetal pregnancies, the daily production rate may exceed 600 mg. Progesterone is synthesized from cholesterol in a two-step enzymatic reaction.

774
01:37:38,563 --> 01:37:49,188
First, cholesterol is converted to pregnenolone within the mitochondria in a reaction catalyzed by cytochrome P450 cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme.

775
01:37:49,188 --> 01:37:58,363
Pregnenolone leaves the mitochondria and is converted to progesterone in the endoplasmic reticulum by 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase.

776
01:37:58,363 --> 01:38:02,013
Progesterone is released immediately by diffusion.

777
01:38:02,013 --> 01:38:11,313
Although the placenta produces a prodigious amount of progesterone, the syncytiotrophoblast has a limited capacity for cholesterol biosynthesis.

778
01:38:11,313 --> 01:38:19,988
The rate-limiting enzyme in its biosynthesis is 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A (HMG-CoA) reductase.

779
01:38:19,988 --> 01:38:28,151
Because of this, the placenta must rely on an exogenous source, that is, maternal cholesterol, for progesterone formation.

780
01:38:28,151 --> 01:38:37,276
The trophoblast preferentially uses LDL cholesterol for progesterone biosynthesis (Simpson, 1979, 1980).

781
01:38:37,276 --> 01:38:44,938
This mechanism differs from placental production of estrogens, which relies principally on fetal adrenal precursors.

782
01:38:44,938 --> 01:38:52,801
Although fetal well-being and placental estrogen production show a relationship, this is not the case for placental progesterone.

783
01:38:52,801 --> 01:39:04,151
Thus, placental endocrine function, including progesterone biosynthesis and formation of protein hormones such as hCG, may persist for weeks after fetal demise.

784
01:39:04,151 --> 01:39:11,138
The metabolic clearance rate of progesterone in pregnant women is similar to that found in men and nonpregnant women.

785
01:39:11,138 --> 01:39:24,913
One metabolite is 5αdihydroprogesterone (5α-DHP), and levels disproportionately rise due to synthesis in syncytiotrophoblast from both placenta-produced progesterone and fetus-derived precursor .

786
01:39:24,913 --> 01:39:34,838
Thus, the concentration ratio of 5α-DHP to progesterone is elevated in pregnancy, although the mechanisms for this are incompletely defined.

787
01:39:34,838 --> 01:39:42,576
Progesterone also is converted to the potent mineralocorticoid deoxycorticosterone in pregnant women and in the fetus.

788
01:39:42,576 --> 01:39:49,426
The concentration of deoxycorticosterone is strikingly higher in both maternal and fetal compartments .

789
01:39:49,426 --> 01:40:00,076
The extraadrenal formation of deoxycorticosterone from circulating progesterone accounts for most of its production in pregnancy (Casey, 1982a,b).

790
01:40:00,076 --> 01:40:03,276
# Placental Estrogen Production.

791
01:40:03,276 --> 01:40:11,538
During the first 2 to 4 weeks or pregnancy, rising hCG levels maintain production of estradiol in the corpus luteum.

792
01:40:11,538 --> 01:40:18,438
Ovarian production of both progesterone and estrogens drops significantly by the 7th week of pregnancy.

793
01:40:18,438 --> 01:40:22,488
At this time, there is a luteal–placental transition.

794
01:40:22,488 --> 01:40:38,863
Subsequently, more than half of estrogen entering maternal circulation is produced in the placenta, and it produces a continually increasing magnitude of estrogen (MacDonald, 1965a; Siiteri, 1963, 1966).

795
01:40:38,863 --> 01:40:52,813
Near term, normal human pregnancy is a hyperestrogenic state, and syncytiotrophoblast is producing estrogen in amounts equivalent to that produced in one day by the ovaries of no fewer than 1000 ovulatory women.

796
01:40:52,813 --> 01:40:58,038
This hyperestrogenic state terminates abruptly after delivery of the placenta.

797
01:40:58,038 --> 01:41:07,176
Biosynthesis In human trophoblast, neither cholesterol nor in turn progesterone can serve as precursor for estrogen biosynthesis.

798
01:41:07,176 --> 01:41:16,838
This is because steroid 17αhydroxylase/17,20-lyase (CYP17A1) is not expressed in the human placenta.

799
01:41:16,838 --> 01:41:26,001
This essential enzyme converts 17-OH progesterone (a C21 steroid) to androstenedione, which is a C19

800
01:41:26,164 --> 01:41:29,289
steroid and an estrogen precursor.

801
01:41:29,239 --> 01:41:35,689
Consequently, conversion of C21 steroids to C19 steroids is not possible.

802
01:41:35,689 --> 01:41:46,851
However, dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) and its sulfate (DHEA-S) are also C19 steroids and are produced by maternal and fetal adrenal glands.

803
01:41:46,851 --> 01:41:51,651
These two steroids can serve as estrogen precursors in the placenta .

804
01:41:51,651 --> 01:42:02,114
Ryan (1959a) found that the placenta had an exceptionally high capacity to convert appropriate C19 steroids to estrone and estradiol.

805
01:42:02,114 --> 01:42:15,314
The conversion of DHEA-S to estradiol requires placental expression of four key enzymes that are located principally in syncytiotrophoblast (Bonenfant, 2000; Salido, 1990).

806
01:42:15,314 --> 01:42:24,564
First, the placenta expresses high levels of steroid sulfatase (STS), which converts the conjugated DHEA-S to DHEA.

807
01:42:24,564 --> 01:42:34,226
DHEA is then acted upon by 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 (3βHSD) to produce androstenedione.

808
01:42:34,226 --> 01:42:49,064
Cytochrome P450 aromatase (CYP19) then converts androstenedione to estrone, which is then converted to estradiol by 17βhydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 (17βHSD1).

809
01:42:49,064 --> 01:42:51,714
Figure 5-17.

810
01:42:51,714 --> 01:42:56,776
Schematic presentation of estrogen biosynthesis in the human placenta.

811
01:42:56,776 --> 01:43:13,326
Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-S) is secreted in prodigious amounts by the fetal adrenal glands, and a portion is converted to 16α-hydroxydehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (16OH-DHEA-S) in the fetal liver.

812
01:43:13,326 --> 01:43:23,414
DHEA-S and 16OH-DHEA-S are converted in the placenta to the estrogens 17βestradiol (E2) and estriol (E3).

813
01:43:23,414 --> 01:43:27,264
These estrogens then enter the maternal circulation.

814
01:43:27,264 --> 01:43:34,539
Near term, half of E2 is derived from fetal adrenal DHEA-S and half from maternal DHEA-S.

815
01:43:34,539 --> 01:43:44,214
On the other hand, 90 percent of E3 in the placenta arises from fetal 16OH-DHEA-S and only 10 percent from all other sources.

816
01:43:44,214 --> 01:44:12,551
3βHSD1 = 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1; 17βHSD1 = 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1; CYP17 = steroid 17α-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase; LDL = low-density lipoprotein; SCC = cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme; StAR = steroidogenic acute regulatory protein.

817
01:44:12,551 --> 01:44:21,264
DHEA-S is the major precursor of estrogens in pregnancy (Baulieu, 1963; Siiteri, 1963).

818
01:44:21,264 --> 01:44:31,326
However, maternal adrenal glands do not produce sufficient amounts of DHEA-S to account for more than a fraction of total placental estrogen biosynthesis.

819
01:44:31,326 --> 01:44:38,989
The fetal adrenal glands are quantitatively the most important source of placental estrogen precursors in human pregnancy.

820
01:44:38,989 --> 01:44:49,351
Thus, estrogen production during pregnancy reflects the unique interactions among fetal adrenal glands, fetal liver, placenta, and maternal adrenal glands.

821
01:44:49,351 --> 01:44:57,939
Directional Secretion Of estradiol and estriol formed in syncytiotrophoblast, >90 percent enters maternal plasma .

822
01:44:57,939 --> 01:45:08,876
Of placental progesterone production, ≥85 percent enters maternal plasma, and little maternal progesterone crosses the placenta to the fetus .

823
01:45:08,876 --> 01:45:15,114
This directional secretion stems from the architecture of hemochorioendothelial placentation.

824
01:45:15,114 --> 01:45:20,976
Steroids produced in the syncytiotrophoblast are secreted directly into maternal blood.

825
01:45:20,976 --> 01:45:29,726
To reach the fetus, they must first traverse the cytotrophoblast layer and then enter the stroma of the villous core and then fetal capillaries.

826
01:45:29,726 --> 01:45:34,351
From either of these spaces, steroids can reenter the syncytium.

827
01:45:34,351 --> 01:45:43,289
The net result of this hemochorial arrangement is that entry of steroids into the maternal circulation is substantially greater than that into fetal blood.

828
01:45:43,289 --> 01:45:46,064
Fetal Adrenal Gland–Placental.

829
01:45:46,064 --> 01:45:47,989
Interactions.

830
01:45:47,989 --> 01:45:55,339
As noted, the fetal adrenal gland is a vital source of steroid precursors for placental estrogen synthesis.

831
01:45:55,339 --> 01:46:00,114
This fetal gland is remarkable both morphologically and functionally.

832
01:46:00,114 --> 01:46:03,564
At term, its mass exceeds that in adults .

833
01:46:03,564 --> 01:46:12,039
More than 85 percent of the fetal gland is composed of a unique fetal zone, which has a great capacity for steroid biosynthesis.

834
01:46:12,039 --> 01:46:22,026
Its daily steroid production near term is 100 to 200 mg/d, which exceeds the adult steroid secretion of 30 to 40 mg/d.

835
01:46:22,026 --> 01:46:26,701
The unique fetal zone subsequently regresses in the first year of life.

836
01:46:26,701 --> 01:46:35,601
In addition to responding to ACTH from the fetal brain, fetal adrenal gland growth is influenced by factors secreted by the placenta.

837
01:46:35,601 --> 01:46:40,801
This is exemplified by the continued adrenal gland growth throughout gestation

838
01:46:40,972 --> 01:46:46,084
and by its rapid involution immediately after birth and placenta delivery.

839
01:46:46,034 --> 01:46:49,322
# Placental Estriol Synthesis.

840
01:46:49,322 --> 01:46:53,897
Estradiol is the primary placental estrogen product at term.

841
01:46:53,897 --> 01:47:04,434
In addition, significant levels of estriol and estetrol are found in the maternal circulation, particularly late in gestation (see Fig. 5-16).

842
01:47:04,434 --> 01:47:16,634
These hydroxylated forms of estrogen derive from the placenta using substrates formed by the combined efforts of the fetal adrenal gland and fetal liver enzymes (see Fig. 5-17).

843
01:47:16,634 --> 01:47:27,897
For this, high levels of hepatic 16α-hydroxylase act on adrenal-derived steroids (MacDonald, 1965b; Ryan, 1959b).

844
01:47:27,897 --> 01:47:45,322
Thus, the disproportionate rise in estriol formation during pregnancy is accounted for by placental synthesis from plasma-borne 16-OH-DHEA-S. Near term, the fetus produces 90 percent of placental estriol and estetrol precursors in normal human pregnancy.

845
01:47:45,322 --> 01:47:51,397
Thus, in the past, levels of these steroids were used as an indicator of fetal wellbeing.

846
01:47:51,397 --> 01:47:54,859
# Fetal Adrenal Steroid Precursor.

847
01:47:54,859 --> 01:47:59,572
Cholesterol is the precursor for fetal adrenal steroidogenesis.

848
01:47:59,572 --> 01:48:10,097
Here, the steroid biosynthesis rate is so great that its steroidogenesis alone is equivalent to a fourth of the total daily LDL cholesterol turnover in adults.

849
01:48:10,097 --> 01:48:20,572
Fetal adrenal glands synthesize cholesterol from acetate, and all enzymes involved in this biosynthesis are elevated compared with those of the adult adrenal gland .

850
01:48:20,572 --> 01:48:26,809
Thus, the de novo cholesterol synthesis rate by fetal adrenal tissue is extremely high.

851
01:48:26,809 --> 01:48:33,047
Even so, it is insufficient to account for the steroids produced by fetal adrenal glands.

852
01:48:33,047 --> 01:48:45,747
Therefore, cholesterol must be assimilated from the fetal circulation and mainly from LDL produced in the fetal liver (Carr, 1980, 1984; Simpson, 1979).

853
01:48:45,747 --> 01:48:49,797
# Fetal Conditions Affecting Estrogen Production.

854
01:48:49,797 --> 01:48:59,984
Several fetal disorders alter the availability of substrate for placental steroid synthesis and thus highlight the interdependence of fetal development and placental function.

855
01:48:59,984 --> 01:49:05,747
Fetal demise is followed by a striking reduction in maternal urinary estrogen levels.

856
01:49:05,747 --> 01:49:14,934
Similarly, after ligation of the umbilical cord with the fetus and placenta left in situ, placental estrogen production declines markedly .

857
01:49:14,934 --> 01:49:20,722
However, as previously discussed, placental progesterone production is maintained.

858
01:49:20,722 --> 01:49:30,547
These observations indicate that an important source of precursors for placental estrogen—but not for progesterone—biosynthesis derive from the fetus.

859
01:49:30,547 --> 01:49:41,684
Anencephalic fetuses have markedly atrophic adrenal glands due to absent hypothalamic and pituitary structures that would otherwise release ACTH for adrenal stimulation.

860
01:49:41,684 --> 01:49:52,997
In the absence of the adrenal cortical fetal zone development, placental formation of estrogen is severely limited because of diminished availability of C19 steroid precursors.

861
01:49:52,997 --> 01:50:02,159
Indeed, urinary estrogen levels in women pregnant with an anencephalic fetus approximate only 10 percent of those found in normal pregnancy .

862
01:50:02,159 --> 01:50:15,647
With an anencephalic fetus, almost all estrogens produced arise from placental use of maternal plasma DHEA-S. Fetal adrenal cortical hypoplasia occurs in perhaps 1 in 12,500 births .

863
01:50:15,647 --> 01:50:22,859
Estrogen production in these pregnancies is also limited, which suggests the absence of C19 precursors.

864
01:50:22,859 --> 01:50:30,484
Fetal–placental sulfatase deficiency is associated with very low estrogen levels in otherwise normal pregnancies .

865
01:50:30,484 --> 01:50:43,122
Namely, sulfatase deficiency precludes the hydrolysis of C19 steroid sulfates, the first enzymatic step in the placental use of these circulating prehormones for estrogen biosynthesis.

866
01:50:43,122 --> 01:50:48,922
This deficiency is an X-linked disorder, and thus all affected fetuses are male.

867
01:50:48,922 --> 01:50:56,422
Its estimated frequency is 1 case in 2000 to 5000 births and is associated with delayed labor onset.

868
01:50:56,422 --> 01:51:02,009
It also is associated with development of ichthyosis in affected males later in life .

869
01:51:02,009 --> 01:51:14,859
Fetal–placental aromatase deficiency is a rare autosomal recessive disorder in which individuals cannot synthesize endogenous estrogens (Grumbach, 2011; Simpson, 2000).

870
01:51:14,859 --> 01:51:26,397
Fetal adrenal DHEA-S is converted in the placenta to androstenedione, but in cases of placental aromatase deficiency, androstenedione cannot be converted to estradiol.

871
01:51:26,397 --> 01:51:27,884
Rather,

872
01:51:28,036 --> 01:51:39,461
androgen metabolites of DHEA produced in the placenta, including androstenedione and some testosterone, are secreted into the maternal or fetal circulation, or both.

873
01:51:39,411 --> 01:51:47,248
This can virilize the mother and the female fetus (Belgorosky, 2009; Harada, 1992).

874
01:51:47,248 --> 01:51:56,286
Trisomy 21—Down syndrome—screening searches for abnormal levels of hCG, alpha-fetoprotein, and other analytes .

875
01:51:56,286 --> 01:52:02,798
Of these, serum unconjugated estriol levels can be low in women with Down syndrome fetuses .

876
01:52:02,798 --> 01:52:10,273
This likely stems from inadequate formation of C19 steroids in the adrenal glands of these trisomic fetuses.

877
01:52:10,273 --> 01:52:19,398
Fetal erythroblastosis in some cases of severe fetal D-antigen alloimmunization can lead to elevated maternal plasma estrogen levels.

878
01:52:19,398 --> 01:52:26,886
A suspected cause is the greater placental mass from hypertrophy, which can be seen with such fetal hemolytic anemia .

879
01:52:26,886 --> 01:52:39,036
Complete hydatidiform mole and gestational trophoblastic neoplasias lack a fetus and also a fetal adrenal source of C19 steroid precursors for trophoblast estrogen biosynthesis.

880
01:52:39,036 --> 01:52:52,511
Placental estrogen formation is consequently limited to the use of C19 steroids from the maternal plasma, and thus estradiol is principally produced (MacDonald, 1964, 1966).

881
01:52:52,511 --> 01:52:56,661
# Maternal Conditions Affecting Estrogen Production.

882
01:52:56,661 --> 01:53:01,486
Maternal conditions can likewise affect placental estrogen production.

883
01:53:01,486 --> 01:53:08,411
Glucocorticoid treatment can inhibit ACTH secretion from the maternal and fetal pituitary glands.

884
01:53:08,411 --> 01:53:18,673
This diminishes maternal and fetal adrenal secretion of the placental estrogen precursor DHEA-S and leads to a striking reduction in placental estrogen formation.

885
01:53:18,673 --> 01:53:26,298
With Addison disease, pregnant women show lower estrogen levels, principally estrone and estradiol levels .

886
01:53:26,298 --> 01:53:37,673
However, the fetal adrenal gland contribution to estriol synthesis, particularly in later pregnancy, is quantitatively much more important than that of the maternal adrenal gland.

887
01:53:37,673 --> 01:53:43,611
Maternal androgen-producing tumors can present the placenta with elevated androgen levels.

888
01:53:43,611 --> 01:53:50,298
Fortunately, placenta is extraordinarily efficient in the aromatization of C19 steroids.

889
01:53:50,298 --> 01:53:59,486
For example, virtually all androstenedione entering the intervillous space is taken up by syncytiotrophoblast and converted to estradiol .

890
01:53:59,486 --> 01:54:07,836
None of the C19 steroid enters the fetus, and a female fetus is rarely virilized by a maternal androgen-secreting tumor.

891
01:54:07,836 --> 01:54:21,973
Indeed, virilized female fetuses of women with an androgenproducing tumor may be cases in which a nonaromatizable C19 steroid androgen is produced by the tumor—for example, 5α-dihydrotestosterone.

892
01:54:21,973 --> 01:54:31,673
Alternatively, tumor-derived testosterone could be produced very early in pregnancy in amounts that exceed the placental aromatase capacity at that time.

